= Chemistry
{wiki}

Chemistry connects the composition and structure of <chemical substances> to their properties and transformations. Its central surprise is that rearranging the same <chemical atoms> can produce a different substance: <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> can become <water> without changing which <chemical elements> are present. <Chemical bonds> explain the connections; <chemical reactions> describe the transformations; <stoichiometry> makes quantitative predictions from the bookkeeping.

This gives a useful route from an invisible world to measurable quantities. A <molecule> has a definite composition, such as two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom> in <water>. A sample contains an enormous number of such <molecules>. Counting them through <amount of substance>, measured in <moles>, lets the same small-number ratios describe a macroscopic sample. The beauty is that the bookkeeping survives this huge change of scale.

= Molecule
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A molecule consists of two or more <chemical atoms> joined by <chemical bonds>, with zero net <electric charge>. This follows the terminology of the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/M04002][IUPAC Gold Book, molecule]{ref}. The <continuum approximation> uses bulk fields in place of individual molecular positions and motions.

= Molecular geometry
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

<Molecular geometry> describes the three-dimensional arrangement of the <atomic nuclei> in a <molecule>, including <bond angles> and distances. A <Lewis structure> gives connections and electron bookkeeping; it does not by itself fix the spatial arrangement. The <VSEPR model> supplies a useful first prediction for many simple <molecules>.

= Bond angle
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{title2=$\theta$}

A <bond angle> is the <angle> between two <chemical bonds> sharing a central <chemical atom>. With nonzero displacement <vectors> $u,v$ from that nucleus to its two neighbours, $\cos\theta=(u\cdot v)/(\|u\|\|v\|)$, using the <dot product> and <Euclidean norm>. The angle in a flat drawing need not equal the actual three-dimensional <bond angle>.

= Tetrahedral molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

Four neighbouring <chemical atoms> lie around a central <chemical atom> in a tetrahedral arrangement. In the regular idealization, their displacement directions point toward the vertices of a <regular tetrahedron>. <Methane> is the standard <VSEPR model> example.

= Trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

Three neighbouring <chemical atoms> form a triangular base, with the central <chemical atom> outside its plane. <Ammonia> provides the standard <VSEPR model> example: three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair> occupy four <electron domains>.

= Bent molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

A three-atom <molecule> has a central <chemical atom> joined to two others with a <bond angle> below $180^\circ$. <Water> is an example. Counting only its two neighbours would miss the role of its two <lone pairs> in the <VSEPR model>.

= Nucleoside
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

A <nucleoside> combines a <nucleobase> with a <sugar>, without the <phosphate> groups of a <nucleotide>. <Adenosine> is the <nucleoside> built from <adenine> and <ribose>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: nucleosides and nucleotides]{ref}.

= Adenosine
{parent=Nucleoside}
{wiki}

<Adenosine> consists of <adenine> joined to <ribose>. Adding one, two, or three <phosphate> groups at the <ribose>'s $5'$ position gives <AMP>, <ADP>, or <ATP>, respectively. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the components of nucleotides]{ref}.

= Nucleotide
{parent=Molecule}

A <nucleotide> contains a <nucleobase>, a <sugar>, and one or more <phosphate> groups. In <DNA> and <RNA>, linked <nucleotides> form a <polymer> whose <nucleotide sequence> carries information. The <sugar> is <deoxyribose> in <DNA> and <ribose> in <RNA>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleotide][NHGRI: nucleotide]{ref}.

= Adenosine triphosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{ATP}$}

= ATP
{c}
{synonym}

<ATP> is a <nucleotide> consisting of <adenosine>, itself <adenine> joined to <ribose>, carrying three <phosphate> groups. From the <ribose> outward, these groups are labelled $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$. <ATP> connects <energy>-supplying processes to <energy coupling> for synthesis and <active transport>; the same carrier can be regenerated and reused. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: ATP composition and recycling]{ref}.

\Image[atp-adp-phosphate-cycle.png]
{title=<ATP> has three <phosphate> groups; <ADP> has two, and regeneration requires <energy> input}
{description=Original component schematic, rather than a molecular structure or a <reaction mechanism>. Lines indicate component order and omit connecting <oxygen> <chemical atoms>, <electric charges>, and three-dimensional geometry. Both sides retain <adenine> and <ribose>; <hydrolysis> consumes <water>, whereas the reverse net <chemical reaction> forms <water>. The <energy> labels describe transfers, not extra material <reactants> or <reaction products>.}

The net <hydrolysis> commonly used in <energy coupling> is
$$
\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i.
$$
Here $\mathrm P_i$ denotes <inorganic phosphate>. At buffered <pH> $7$, this biochemical shorthand groups the appropriate <protonation> states into pools and leaves <proton> exchange with the <buffer solution> implicit. An explicit, charge-balanced choice is $\mathrm{ATP}^{4-}+\mathrm{H_2O}\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}^{3-}+\mathrm{HPO_4}^{2-}+\mathrm H^+$, without implying these are the only <chemical species> present. See the \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/][IUBMB recommendations for biochemical thermodynamics]{ref}.

The quoted textbook biochemical <standard state> value is approximately
$$
\Delta G^{\circ\prime}=-30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
This convention uses fixed <pH> $7$, <temperature> $298.15\,\mathrm K$ ($25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$), reference <pressure> $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ ($1\,\mathrm{bar}$), unit reference <thermodynamic activities> for dissolved <reactants> and <reaction products>, and <water> <thermodynamic activity> approximated by one. The prime indicates the fixed-<pH> biochemical convention. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: standard ATP hydrolysis value]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy][Biology 2e, 6.2: biochemical standard conditions]{ref}. This is a reference value, rather than a universal value inside a <biological cell>; precise tables must also specify <chemical solution> conditions and any <magnesium> <ion> binding convention. Different reference conventions and data treatments give different tabulated values, as discussed in \a[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3299200/][Li et al., 2010: a database of thermodynamic quantities for biochemical reactions]{ref}.

An <exergonic reaction> has a negative change in <Gibbs free energy>, so the overall <ATP> <hydrolysis> is favourable under those reference conditions. Breaking a <chemical bond> by itself costs <energy>; forming new <chemical bonds> can release <energy>. The net change compares all <reactants> with all <reaction products>, including their interactions with <water> and the <entropy> contribution. Calling the phosphate connection a "high-energy bond" describes a large favourable change in <Gibbs free energy> on <hydrolysis>, rather than <energy> released by bond breaking alone. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-5-strengths-of-ionic-and-covalent-bonds][OpenStax Chemistry 2e, 7.5: breaking and forming bonds]{ref} and \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/][Cooper, The Cell: ATP and metabolic energy]{ref}.

Composition changes the driving force. At the same fixed <pH>, <temperature>, <pressure>, and binding convention, write the molar reaction change as
$$
\Delta G=\Delta G^{\circ\prime}+RT\ln Q,\qquad
Q=\frac{a_{\mathrm{ADP}}a_{\mathrm P_i}}{a_{\mathrm{ATP}}}.
$$
Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, $T$ the absolute <temperature>, and $a$ a <dimensionless> <thermodynamic activity> for a biochemical pool. Constant <water> <thermodynamic activity> is absorbed into the reference term; fixed <pH> accounts for buffered <protons>. The shorthand $\Delta G$ means transformed molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> change, $\Delta_rG'$, rather than a finite sample's total change. The <natural logarithm> acts on a <dimensionless> <reaction quotient>, never on <concentrations> with units. See the \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/][IUBMB biochemical thermodynamics recommendations]{ref} and the general <equilibrium constant> relation.

For a stipulated ideal dilute <chemical solution>, let $a_i=[i]/c^\circ$, with $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. Choose $[\mathrm{ATP}]=5\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm{ADP}]=0.5\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, and $[\mathrm P_i]=1\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, ignoring <activity coefficient> corrections and <magnesium> binding. These are illustrative inputs, not a claim about a particular <biological cell>. Then
$$
Q=\frac{(0.0005)(0.001)}{0.005}=10^{-4},\qquad
RT=(0.008314462618)(298.15)=2.47895703\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},
$$
and
$$
\Delta G=-30.5+2.47895703\ln(10^{-4})
\approx-53.332\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Increasing $Q$ tenfold raises $\Delta G$ by $RT\ln10\approx5.708\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at this <temperature>. Thus the <ATP> to <ADP> ratio and available <inorganic phosphate> matter, even with the same <standard state> value. These are local molar predictions at the stipulated composition, rather than a constant total yield as an isolated sample reacts.

= Adenosine diphosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{ADP}$}

= ADP
{c}
{synonym}

<ADP> contains the same <adenosine> unit as <ATP>, with two <phosphate> groups instead of three. One <ATP> converted by the stated <hydrolysis> gives one <ADP> and one <inorganic phosphate>: $3=2+1$ accounts for the <phosphate> groups while <adenine> and <ribose> are retained. <ADP> is therefore a reusable material carrier, rather than <ATP> with its entire structure discarded.

Regeneration reverses the net <chemical reaction>:
$$
\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i\longrightarrow\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}.
$$
At the same stipulated reference conditions its $\Delta G^{\circ\prime}$ is $+30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, the negative of the <hydrolysis> value. It is an <endergonic reaction> and requires <energy> input through <energy coupling>; a <catalyst> alone cannot supply that input. Recycling the material carrier does not recycle the expended driving <energy>. <Cellular respiration> and <light-dependent reactions> supply routes for regeneration. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/][Cooper, The Cell: ATP generation and energy coupling]{ref}.

<ADP> is a <nucleotide> with two <phosphate> groups. Formation of <ATP> from <ADP> and <phosphate> couples to <energy> supply in <light-dependent reactions>; the <Calvin cycle> returns <ADP> after using <ATP>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling ATP and ADP]{ref}.

= Adenosine monophosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{AMP}$}

= AMP
{c}
{synonym}

<AMP> is <adenosine> carrying one <phosphate> group at the <ribose>'s $5'$ position. The count distinguishes <AMP> from <ADP> with two and <ATP> with three; <adenosine> itself has none. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/][Cooper, The Cell: ATP, ADP, and AMP]{ref}.

= Nucleotide base
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

= Nucleobase
{synonym}

= Adenine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm A$}
{wiki}

= Guanine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm G$}
{wiki}

= Cytosine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Thymine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm T$}
{wiki}

= Uracil
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm U$}
{wiki}

= Quantum chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Electron density
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{title2=$n(\mathbf r)$}

The electron density describes the expected number of <electrons> per unit <volume> at position $\mathbf r$. For a system with a fixed number $N$ of <electrons>, $\int n(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r=N$. A <Lewis structure> allocates electron counts without specifying this spatial density.

= Electron configuration
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

An <electron configuration> records the occupation of <electron shells> and <electron subshells> by <electrons>. For neutral <sodium> in its <ground state>, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^1$ gives $2+2+6+1=11$ <electrons>; adding the occupations within each <electron shell> gives $(2,8,1)$. A superscript counts <electrons>, rather than multiplying the preceding labels. Removing the outer <electron> gives the <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ with $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6$, or $(2,8)$. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electron configurations of atoms and ions]{ref}.

= Electron shell
{parent=Electron configuration}
{title2=$n$}
{wiki}

An <electron shell> collects the allowed electronic <quantum states> sharing a <principal quantum number> $n$. Its occupancy is the number of <electrons> assigned to those states in an <electron configuration>. An <electron shell> is not a material surface or a prescribed orbit.

= Electron subshell
{parent=Electron shell}

An <electron subshell> groups the allowed electronic <quantum states> within an <electron shell> by their <orbital angular momentum> quantum number $\ell$. Labels $s$ and $p$ mean $\ell=0$ and $\ell=1$, respectively; their capacities are two and six <electrons>. The leading number in $2p$ identifies the <electron shell>, while $p$ identifies the <electron subshell>.

= Atomic orbital
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi_{n\ell m}(\mathbf r)$}

An atomic orbital is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> used to describe an <electron>'s state in a <chemical atom>. For a one-electron <hydrogen atom>, orbitals can be exact <energy eigenstates>; descriptions of many-electron atoms instead use an orbital approximation. A normalized atomic orbital has $\int|\phi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$, and the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\phi|^2$. It is a function extending through space, not a prescribed classical orbit. \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 6.3: atomic orbitals and the wavefunction description]{ref}

The <linear combination of atomic orbitals> combines these single-centre functions to approximate a <molecular orbital>. The <orbital overlap> measures their <inner product>, and their relative <quantum phase> controls the <interference> shown in <molecular orbital theory>.

= Molecular orbital theory
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

<Molecular orbital theory> describes the electronic arrangement of a <molecule> using <molecular orbitals>: one-<electron> spatial <wavefunctions> extending over the combined <atomic nuclei>. Filling <bonding molecular orbitals> can stabilize a <chemical bond>; filling <antibonding molecular orbitals> opposes that stabilization. A <molecular orbital> is a building block of a many-<electron> description, rather than the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf][Robert W. Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 1–4: molecular orbitals and the LCAO approximation]{ref}.

First make the <interference> visible with an exactly normalized one-dimensional toy. Replace the three-dimensional $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom> by two <Gaussian functions> centred at $x=\pm a$, with <dimensionless> $x$ and $a>0$:
$$
\phi_A(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x+a)^2/2},\qquad
\phi_B(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x-a)^2/2},\qquad
S=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_A\phi_B\,dx=e^{-a^2}.
$$
The normalized sum and difference are $\psi_\pm=(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)/\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}$, following the same <linear combination of atomic orbitals> algebra. At $a=1$, the <orbital overlap> is $S=e^{-1}\approx0.367879$. At the midpoint, $\phi_A(0)=\phi_B(0)\approx0.455581$, giving
$$
\psi_+(0)\approx0.550879,\qquad |\psi_+(0)|^2\approx0.303468,\qquad
\psi_-(0)=|\psi_-(0)|^2=0.
$$
Both <probability densities> have total <integral> one: <interference> redistributes <probability>, rather than creating an extra <electron>. These are toy <wavefunctions>, not actual <hydrogen atom> orbitals or predictions of <molecular hydrogen> <energies>.

\Image[molecular-orbital-interference.png]
{title=Normalized one-dimensional <Gaussian function> toy: signed <probability amplitudes> add or cancel before their squares give <probability densities>}
{description=Centres at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ give <orbital overlap> $S=e^{-1}$. The difference has a zero at the midpoint; its negative lobe does not carry positive <electric charge>. All density curves shown are normalized on the line, rather than in three-dimensional space.}

Now fill the two $1s$-derived <molecular orbitals> in the minimal model. The lower <bonding molecular orbital> is labelled $\sigma_g$; the upper <antibonding molecular orbital> is $\sigma_u^*$. The <Pauli exclusion principle> permits at most two <electrons> in each spatial <molecular orbital>, with opposite <spin> projections along a chosen axis. For the lowest <electron configuration> in this model, fill $\sigma_g$ first. The subscripts $g$ and $u$ denote <even> and <odd> behaviour under inversion through the midpoint; the star marks an <antibonding molecular orbital>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 8.4: occupancy and bond order]{ref} and \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf][Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–3: bonding, antibonding, and symmetry labels]{ref}.

With $N_b$ <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> and $N_a$ in <antibonding molecular orbitals>, the model's <bond order> is $b=(N_b-N_a)/2$. The three elementary counts are:
* <Molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$: two <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2$ and $b=(2-0)/2=1$.
* The <hydrogen> molecular <ion> $\mathrm H_2^+$: one <electron> gives $(\sigma_g)^1$ and $b=(1-0)/2=1/2$.
* Two neutral <helium> <chemical atoms>, $\mathrm{He}_2$: four <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2(\sigma_u^*)^2$ and $b=(2-2)/2=0$ in this minimal $1s$ model.

\Image[molecular-orbital-occupancy.png]
{title=Schematic <molecular orbital> occupancy for $\mathrm H_2$, $\mathrm H_2^+$, and $\mathrm{He}_2$: <bond orders> one, one half, and zero}
{description=Each horizontal line is one spatial <molecular orbital>; paired arrows represent opposite <spin> projections. Vertical positions show ordering only, with arbitrary gaps rather than measured <energies> or a common scale across species.}

The zero <bond order> says this minimal model gives no net <covalent bond> for $\mathrm{He}_2$. It does not rule out every bound pair of <helium> <chemical atoms>: a very weak <van der Waals force> can bind a neutral <helium> dimer, which has been observed experimentally. See \a[https://www.atom.uni-frankfurt.de/publications/files/ZellerPNAS-2016.pdf][Zeller et al., Imaging the He2 quantum halo state using a free electron laser, PNAS 113, 14651–14655 (2016), p. 14651: van der Waals binding]{ref} and \a[https://scholarworks.gvsu.edu/chm_articles/6/][Luo et al., The weakest bond: Experimental observation of helium dimer, Journal of Chemical Physics 98, 3564–3567 (1993)]{ref}. The <bond order> count does not include that weak binding mechanism.

The useful surprise is that sharing alone does not determine a <chemical bond>: the relative <quantum phase> and the occupancy matter together. The same two shapes explain why adding an <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> increases the model's <bond order>, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> decreases it. <Molecular orbital theory> also accounts for the unpaired <electrons> and <paramagnetism> of <molecular oxygen>, which its all-paired <Lewis structure> misses. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory][Chemistry 2e, 8.4: the molecular orbital description of oxygen]{ref}.

= Molecular orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$\psi(\mathbf r)$}
{wiki}

A <molecular orbital> is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> for the combined <atomic nuclei> of a <molecule>. For a normalized <molecular orbital>, the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\psi(\mathbf r)|^2$, with $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$. The <molecular orbital> is the amplitude, rather than its squared <modulus> or a classical trajectory.

In the independent-orbital approximation, a doubly occupied <molecular orbital> contributes $2|\psi|^2$ to the <electron> number density, whose <integral> is two. An empty <molecular orbital> contributes zero, even though its shape is still part of the model. The <molecular hydrogen> example therefore uses one occupied spatial <molecular orbital> for two <electrons>, distinguished by their <spins>. A negative real <probability amplitude> means a relative <quantum phase> of $\pi$, not positive <electric charge>; multiplying the whole <wavefunction> by $-1$ changes only its <global phase>.

= Bonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_g$}
{wiki}

= Bonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying a <bonding molecular orbital> favours a <chemical bond>. For the two equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the sum $\sigma_g=\sigma_+$ has constructive <interference> between the <protons>. The resulting <electron> density in that region helps stabilize the combined system. Its lower <energy> in this model follows from the <quantum Hamiltonian>, not from the plus sign alone; see \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf][Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2]{ref}.

For example, $(\sigma_g)^2$ in <molecular hydrogen> supplies $N_b=2$, hence <bond order> one when $N_a=0$. The sum in the <molecular orbital theory> toy has midpoint <probability density> $0.303468$, whereas the difference has zero there. The normalization factors must be included in this comparison.

= Antibonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_u^*$}
{wiki}

= Antibonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying an <antibonding molecular orbital> opposes a <chemical bond>. For equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the difference $\sigma_u^*=\sigma_-$ vanishes on the plane halfway between the <protons>: every point there is equally far from both centres, so the amplitudes cancel. This nodal plane removes the contribution of this <molecular orbital> to <electron> density on that plane; it does not make an entire finite region empty. The $1s$-derived <antibonding molecular orbital> lies above its <bonding molecular orbital> partner in <energy>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf][Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2]{ref}.

In the $\mathrm{He}_2$ minimal model, $(\sigma_u^*)^2$ adds $N_a=2$ and cancels the <bond order> contribution of $(\sigma_g)^2$. Opposite signs on the two lobes describe <probability amplitudes>, while both lobes have nonnegative <probability density> after squaring. The star is an orbital label, not an extra <electron> or an <electric charge>.

= Linear combination of atomic orbitals
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{wiki}

= LCAO
{c}
{synonym}

The <LCAO> approximation builds a <molecular orbital> as a <linear combination> of single-centre <atomic orbitals>. For two equivalent normalized real $1s$ functions $\phi_A$ and $\phi_B$, choose their signs positive and define their <orbital overlap> $S$. At nonzero separation, $0<S<1$, and the normalized combinations are
$$
\sigma_\pm(\mathbf r)=\frac{\phi_A(\mathbf r)\pm\phi_B(\mathbf r)}{\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}},\qquad
S=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\phi_A(\mathbf r)\phi_B(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r.
$$
Here $\sigma_+=\sigma_g$ and $\sigma_-=\sigma_u^*$. Equal coefficients use the equivalence of the two centres; a general <LCAO> need not have equal coefficients. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf][Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 3–6: LCAO coefficients and overlap integrals]{ref}.

For the <Gaussian function> toy's $a=1$, the two denominators are $\sqrt{2(1+S)}\approx1.654013$ and $\sqrt{2(1-S)}\approx1.124385$. To check <wavefunction normalization>, expand the square: $\int(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)^2=1+1\pm2S$. Dividing by $2(1\pm S)$ gives one. The local <probability density> is
$$
|\sigma_\pm|^2=\frac{\phi_A^2+\phi_B^2\pm2\phi_A\phi_B}{2(1\pm S)}.
$$
The cross term is the <interference>; adding the separate densities first would lose it. These formulas normalize the chosen <linear combination> exactly, while the restricted choice of <atomic orbitals> remains an approximation to an actual <molecular orbital>.

= Orbital overlap
{parent=Linear combination of atomic orbitals}
{title2=$S$}

The <orbital overlap> of normalized real <atomic orbitals> is their <inner product> $S=\int\phi_A\phi_B$. It is a <dimensionless> measure of their common amplitude, not an <electron> count. For the <Gaussian function> toy, increasing $a$ from $1$ to $2$ reduces $S$ from $e^{-1}\approx0.367879$ to $e^{-4}\approx0.018316$. As $S$ tends to zero, both <LCAO> normalization denominators tend to $\sqrt2$.

= Bond order
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$b=(N_b-N_a)/2$}
{wiki}

In the elementary <molecular orbital theory> count, <bond order> is half the excess of <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> over those in <antibonding molecular orbitals>. Thus adding one <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> raises $b$ by $1/2$, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> lowers it by $1/2$. The configurations drawn above give $1$, $1/2$, and $0$. This count summarizes net <covalent bond> occupancy; it is neither a measured <energy> nor a universal test for every kind of binding. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory][Chemistry 2e, 8.4: molecular orbital bond order]{ref}.

= Chemical bond
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical bond> associates <chemical atoms> or groups of <chemical atoms> into a sufficiently stable structure to treat it as a distinct entity. The concept covers connections within <molecules> and in extended solids; see the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/CT07009][IUPAC Gold Book, chemical bond]{ref}. It is useful because composition alone does not specify how the <chemical atoms> are connected, and those connections help determine a <chemical substance>'s properties and possible <chemical reactions>.

In a <covalent bond>, <electrons> are shared between <chemical atoms>. Two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> can form a <molecular hydrogen> <molecule>, with its <electrons> interacting with both <atomic nuclei>. In the usual description of this diatomic <molecule>, the <potential energy> as a function of nuclear separation has a minimum: moving the nuclei too close or too far raises the <energy>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-2-covalent-bonding][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, covalent bonding]{ref}.

Separating bonded <chemical atoms> requires <energy>; forming the corresponding <chemical bonds> releases <energy>. Whether an overall <chemical reaction> releases <energy> depends on both the <chemical bonds> broken and those formed. The lines in the <stoichiometry> diagram indicate connections schematically, rather than literal rods or a sequence of microscopic events.

= Electronegativity
{parent=Chemical bond}
{title2=$\chi$}
{wiki}

= Electronegative
{synonym}

<Electronegativity> describes an atom's tendency to attract <electron density> in a <chemical bond>. Different scales operationalize the comparison differently, so numerical values should be compared on the same scale. The <electronegativity> difference helps explain unequal sharing of <electrons>, distinct from the integer bookkeeping of <formal charge>.\a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E01990][IUPAC Gold Book, electronegativity: Pauling and Mulliken scales]{ref}

= Bond length
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$r$}

A <bond length> describes the separation of the <atomic nuclei> of two bonded <chemical atoms>. Distinguish an equilibrium geometry from an instantaneous or experimentally averaged separation. Methods of determining <molecular geometry> can use different averaging conventions, so reported <bond lengths> need their measurement context. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/BT07003][IUPAC Gold Book: bond length]{ref}. Changing a <bond length> is one possible <reaction coordinate>.

= Partial atomic charge
{parent=Chemical bond}

A partial atomic charge assigns an <electric charge> to a <chemical atom> by partitioning the actual <electron density>. The result depends on the partitioning convention and need not be an integer. It differs from <formal charge>, which divides bonding <electrons> equally in a specified <Lewis structure>.

= Electron pair
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

An <electron pair> consists of two <electrons> with opposite <spins> sharing the same spatial part of their <quantum state>. The <Lewis structure> distinction between a <bond pair> and a <lone pair> records whether that pair is assigned to a connection between <chemical atoms> or to one <chemical atom>.

= Hydrogen bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$}

A <hydrogen bond> is an attractive association involving a <hydrogen> attached to another <chemical atom>, and an accepting atom or group. In the IUPAC 2011 definition, the atom $X$ in $X-\mathrm H$ is more <electronegative> than <hydrogen>, and evidence of bond formation is required. The familiar strong examples use O–H or N–H groups, but restricting the concept to only nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine misses weaker cases. A short distance alone is not the entire definition.\a[https://publications.iupac.org/pac/pdf/2011/pdf/8308x1637.pdf][Arunan et al., Definition of the hydrogen bond, IUPAC Recommendations 2011]{ref}

\Image[water-hydrogen-bond-roles.png]
{title=Two water molecules play different hydrogen-bond roles while retaining their own covalent O–H bonds}

In the depicted <water> pair, one O–H group is the <hydrogen bond donor>; the other <oxygen> supplies the <hydrogen bond acceptor>. The solid O–H line denotes a <covalent bond> within a <water> <molecule>; the dotted H···O line denotes the <hydrogen bond> between them. One <water> <molecule> can play both roles in a larger arrangement because it has O–H groups and <lone pairs>.

This makes <hydrogen bonds> useful for understanding organization beyond a single <molecular formula>. They connect <water> <molecules> and contribute to <protein> folding and recognition between <molecules>. The same types of <chemical atoms> can support different associations depending on which donor and acceptor sites are exposed and how they are oriented. Alberts and colleagues discuss these noncovalent interactions in \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Molecular Biology of the Cell, the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

<Hydrogen bonds> are often directional, with an approximately straight donor–H–acceptor arrangement favourable in familiar cases; strength and preferred geometry depend on the system. The IUPAC definition uses converging experimental or theoretical evidence rather than one universal distance threshold. The author-hosted \a[https://ipc.iisc.ac.in/~ea/iupac/iupac/IUPAC_finalreport_Nov_2009TG.pdf][2009 task-group draft, §§3.1–3.3]{ref} explains the definition and typical criteria leading to the recommendation.

= Hydrogen bond donor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H$}

A <hydrogen bond donor> supplies the attached <hydrogen> participating in a <hydrogen bond>. A group or the <molecule> containing it may be called the donor. The terminology does not mean that the illustrated association has transferred a free <proton>: in the ordinary <water> pair, the O–H <covalent bond> remains.

= Hydrogen bond acceptor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}

A <hydrogen bond acceptor> supplies the electron-rich site toward which the donor's <hydrogen> interacts. An <oxygen> <lone pair> is the familiar <water> example; acceptor sites are not limited to this one kind of <Lewis structure> description. A single <molecule> can contain both a <hydrogen bond donor> and a <hydrogen bond acceptor>.

= Covalent bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <covalent bond> joins <chemical atoms> through shared <electrons>. In the familiar two-centre description, one <bond pair> contributes to a connection between two <atomic nuclei>. Sharing means that the <electrons> belong to the combined arrangement and interact with both <atomic nuclei>; it does not mean that identifiable dots shuttle along drawn paths. The \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01384][IUPAC Gold Book, covalent bond]{ref} emphasizes the concentration of <electrons> between <atomic nuclei> and the resulting attraction and characteristic separation. The pair picture is especially useful for simple <molecules>, but is not a universal description of every <covalent bond>.

A <Lewis structure> turns this picture into practical bookkeeping. Count the <valence electrons>, indicate which <chemical atoms> are connected, and allocate two <electrons> to each line. A <bond pair> is shared; a <lone pair> is assigned to one <chemical atom> without connecting it to another. A double line represents two <bond pairs>. Dots and lines record allocations, rather than positions, trajectories, or a measured three-dimensional arrangement. \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, Lewis symbols and structures]{ref}

For <molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$, each neutral <hydrogen> <chemical atom> supplies one <valence electron>. Thus $1+1=2$ <valence electrons> make one <bond pair>, represented by $\mathrm{H-H}$, with no <lone pairs>. Each <hydrogen> counts the same shared pair when checking its two-electron arrangement. The <molecule> still contains two <electrons>, not four: counting a pair around both endpoints does not create extra <electrons>.

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, neutral <oxygen> supplies six <valence electrons> and the two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> supply one each: $6+1+1=8$. Two O–H connections consume four <electrons> as two <bond pairs>. The remaining $8-4=4$ <electrons> form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. Around <oxygen>, the two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs> count as eight <electrons>; each <hydrogen> counts two. The whole <water> <molecule> has ten <electrons>, because two additional <oxygen> <electrons> are omitted from this <valence electron> drawing.

For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, the total is $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons>: five from <nitrogen> and three from <hydrogen>. Three N–H connections use six <electrons> as three <bond pairs>, leaving two <electrons> as one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Thus <water> and <ammonia> have the same total of eight <valence electrons> but different allocations: two plus two pairs versus three plus one. This small calculation already distinguishes their connectivity without a detailed <quantum mechanics> calculation.

\Image[lewis-electron-pair-counts.png]
{title=<Lewis structures> count <bond pairs> and <lone pairs> in <molecular hydrogen>, <water>, <ammonia>, and <molecular oxygen>; schematic layouts do not specify molecular shapes}

For <molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, $6+6=12$ <valence electrons> give the conventional $\mathrm{O=O}$ <Lewis structure>. Its two <bond pairs> use four <electrons>, leaving eight as four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> consequently counts eight <electrons> locally. However, actual <molecular oxygen> has two unpaired <electrons> and exhibits <paramagnetism>: it is attracted into a stronger <magnetic field>. The all-paired <Lewis structure> cannot capture this observation. <Molecular orbital theory> describes the unpaired <electrons>; the successful counting diagram alone is insufficient. \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, molecular orbital theory]{ref}

Why does sharing produce a stable separation? For <molecular hydrogen>, the electronic arrangement responds as the <atomic nuclei> approach. Their interactions can lower the <energy>, but pushing them too close raises it. A curve of <potential energy> against separation therefore has a <local minimum>, rather than falling forever. Attraction and repulsion balance there. This is a statement about the combined system, not a claim that stationary <electrons> sit between otherwise motionless <atomic nuclei>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-2-covalent-bonding][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, covalent bonding]{ref}

The following <Morse potential> makes those features calculable. Set $x=r/r_e$, where $r$ is separation and $r_e$ its equilibrium value, and let $D$ be the well depth. With the self-chosen parameter $a=2$, define the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
u(x)=\frac{V(r)}{D}=\left(1-e^{-2(x-1)}\right)^2-1,
\qquad f(x)=\frac{F(r)r_e}{D}=-\frac{du}{dx}=4\left(e^{-4(x-1)}-e^{-2(x-1)}\right).
$$
Here $F=-dV/dr$ is the net separating <force>, obtained from the <derivative> of the <potential energy>. Positive $f$ pushes the <chemical atoms> apart; negative $f$ draws them together. These are illustrative curves with $r_e=D=1$ in chosen units, not measured <molecular hydrogen> data or fitted parameters.

\Image[covalent-bond-energy-model.png]
{title=Illustrative <dimensionless> <Morse potential> and its <force>: a stable <local minimum>, short-distance repulsion, and separation toward zero <potential energy>}

At $x=1$, $u=-1$ and $f=0$. The <second derivative> is $u''(1)=8>0$, confirming a <local minimum>. At $x=0.5$, $u=(1-e)^2-1\approx1.9525$; at $x=1.5$, $u=(1-e^{-1})^2-1\approx-0.6004$. As $x$ increases without bound, $u$ approaches zero from below. Thus moving from the well bottom to separated <chemical atoms> requires an increase of $D$ in this model's <potential energy>. An actual <quantum mechanics> dissociation threshold also depends on the initial motion of the <atomic nuclei>, so the well depth alone is not every measured dissociation <energy>.

The <force> panel separates $f$ into a positive term $4e^{-4(x-1)}$ and a negative term $-4e^{-2(x-1)}$. At $x=1$ they are $+4$ and $-4$, cancelling exactly; nearer separations favour the positive term. These are algebraic components of the chosen <Morse potential>, not separately measured microscopic <forces>. Their usefulness is to show how a balance arises from the slope of one <energy> curve.

The beauty of the pair picture is its economy: a few marks summarize connectivity and constrain what a proposed <molecule> can contain. Its historical anchor is Gilbert N. Lewis's 1916 paper \a[https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002][The Atom and the Molecule, Journal of the American Chemical Society 38, 762–785]{ref}, also available as an \a[https://zenodo.org/records/1429068][archived original paper]{ref}. <Lewis structures> remain useful because they answer counting questions quickly; <quantum mechanics> supplies the richer account when <energy>, shape, or unpaired <electrons> matter.

= Phosphodiester bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{wiki}

A <phosphodiester bond> connects two groups through a <phosphate> diester. In a <nucleic acid> backbone, the bridging <phosphate> is connected through <oxygen> to the $3'$ <carbon> position of one <sugar> and the $5'$ <carbon> position of another. These <covalent bonds> connect successive <nucleotides> along a strand; they are distinct from the <hydrogen bonds> between paired <nucleobases>.

= Disulfide bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{title2=$\mathrm{S-S}$}

A disulfide bond is the <covalent bond> between the two adjacent <sulfur> <chemical atoms> in a <disulfide>. A <protein disulfide bond> is a biological example: it can link different portions of a <polypeptide chain> without altering the sequence of its <peptide bonds>. See <ribonuclease A refolding experiment> for a case where breaking and reforming these crosslinks changes <protein structure> and function.

= Lewis structure
{parent=Covalent bond}
{c}
{wiki}

= Lewis formula
{c}
{synonym}

= Lewis dot structure
{c}
{synonym}

Before calculating electronic <energy>, ask a simpler question: where can the available <valence electrons> go? A <Lewis structure> makes that budget visible, and <nitrate> shows why a correct budget can still require several <resonance structures>.

A <Lewis structure> records which <chemical atoms> are connected in a <molecule> or <ion> and how its <valence electrons> are allocated. One line represents one <bond pair>, two lines two <bond pairs>, and three lines three <bond pairs>; two dots beside a <chemical atom> represent one <lone pair>. The marks are a counting language, rather than measured <electron> positions. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03513][IUPAC Gold Book, Lewis formula]{ref}.

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, the budget is $6+2\times1=8$ <valence electrons>. Two O–H lines use four <electrons>; the other four form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons> give three N–H lines and one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Both <molecules> contain four <electron pairs>, but their allocations differ. Each shared <bond pair> is counted once in the total budget, even though both endpoints count it when checking the <octet rule>.

\Image[lewis-water-ammonia.png]
{title=Eight <valence electrons> in each <molecule>: <water> has two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>; <ammonia> has three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>}
{description=Each blue line represents two <electrons>; each orange dot represents one <electron>. The <oxygen> and <nitrogen> each count eight <electrons> locally, while each <hydrogen> counts two. These are schematic <Lewis structures>, not predictions of molecular shape or <electron> trajectories.}

A practical construction method is:
* Add the neutral <chemical atoms>' <valence electron> counts. Add one <electron> for each unit of negative <electric charge>, or subtract one for each unit of positive <electric charge>.
* Choose candidate connections and put one <bond pair> on each. <Hydrogen> normally has one connection in these simple <Lewis structures>.
* Place remaining <electrons> as <lone pairs>, checking the <octet rule>. If an endpoint has eight <electrons> but the central <chemical atom> lacks them, try converting an endpoint <lone pair> into an additional <bond pair>, keeping the total budget fixed.
* Compute every <formal charge> and check its sum against the net <electric charge>. Compare reasonable candidates, including <chemical resonance> when several allocations share the same connections.

For <carbon dioxide>, $4+2\times6=16$ <valence electrons>. Starting with two single C–O connections leaves twelve <electrons>, enough for three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, but only four <electrons> around <carbon>. Converting one <lone pair> from each <oxygen> into a second <bond pair> gives $\mathrm{O=C=O}$. Eight <electrons> are in the four <bond pairs>, and eight are in four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. <Carbon> has no <lone pair>; all three <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>, and all three <formal charges> vanish.

For the <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, the <valence electron> budget is
$$
5+3\times6+1=24.
$$
<Nitrogen> contributes five, the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> contribute eighteen, and the negative <electric charge> adds one <electron>. Start with <nitrogen> connected to each <oxygen> by one <bond pair>: those three lines use six <electrons>. Put the remaining eighteen <electrons> into three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> now satisfies the <octet rule>, but <nitrogen> counts only six <electrons>. Reassign one <oxygen> <lone pair> as a second <bond pair> on its N–O connection. This changes the allocation without adding an <electron>; it applies the construction procedure in \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.3: drawing Lewis structures]{ref}.

The resulting <nitrate> <Lewis structure> has one N=O connection and two N–O connections. Its four <bond pairs> use eight <electrons>, and its eight <lone pairs> use sixteen: $8+16=24$. The doubly connected <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>; each singly connected <oxygen> has three; <nitrogen> has none. All four <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>. Any of the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> can supply the additional <bond pair>, giving the three equivalent <resonance structures> below.

\Image[nitrate-resonance-contributors.png]
{title=Three equivalent <nitrate> <resonance structures>, each with 24 <valence electrons>}
{description=The same labelled <chemical atoms> occupy the same positions in all three <Lewis structures>. Each line counts two <electrons>; each dot counts one. Each drawing has four <bond pairs>, eight <lone pairs>, <formal charge> $+1$ on <nitrogen>, $-1$ on each singly connected <oxygen>, and zero on the doubly connected <oxygen>. The brackets give the net <electric charge>; the arrows indicate <chemical resonance>, not a <chemical reaction> or elapsed time.}

Why not draw three N=O connections to make the <ion> look symmetric? With two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, that drawing still spends $12+12=24$ <electrons> and has the right total <formal charge>, but places twelve <electrons> around <nitrogen>. It fails the <octet rule> for this second-period <chemical element>. Three equivalent <resonance structures> express the symmetry without that invalid local count.

The economy is beautiful: a handful of dots and lines rules out many impossible counts and identifies <lone pairs> that can participate in <acid-base chemistry>. A <Lewis structure> helps choose rational candidate connections before a detailed <quantum chemistry> calculation. It does not specify a complete many-<electron> <wavefunction>, actual <electron density>, or molecular geometry. Adding the separate <VSEPR model>, described in \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity][Chemistry 2e, 7.6: molecular structure and polarity]{ref}, predicts bent <water> and pyramidal <ammonia>; the layouts above alone make no such prediction. <Molecular orbital theory> supplies a richer electronic description, including the unpaired <electrons> of <molecular oxygen> that an all-paired <Lewis structure> misses.

In 1916 Gilbert N. Lewis proposed two-dot notation for the shared <electrons> of a <chemical bond> and fuller dot formulae for the outer <electrons>, explicitly relating a shared pair to the existing single-line notation. See \a[https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002][Lewis, The Atom and the Molecule, JACS 38, 762–785 (1916), pp. 777–778]{ref}, available as an \a[https://zenodo.org/records/1429068][archived original paper]{ref}; the relevant passage is also readable in a \a[https://www.chemteam.info/Chem-History/Lewis-1916/Lewis-1916.html][transcription of the original paper]{ref}. The modern <quantum mechanics> interpretation should be distinguished from his historical model of fixed outer <electrons>.

= Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki=VSEPR_theory}

= VSEPR model
{c}
{synonym}

= VSEPR theory
{c}
{synonym}

The <VSEPR model> predicts approximate <molecular geometry> by arranging the <electron domains> around a central <chemical atom> to reduce their mutual repulsion. Start with a <Lewis structure>, count the domains, and then distinguish the resulting <electron pair geometry> from the positions of the <chemical atoms> themselves. A <lone pair> counts as a domain even though it is not another <chemical atom>.\a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-091-introduction-to-solid-state-chemistry-fall-2018/g9v8zj6VObw_transcript.pdf][MIT 3.091, lecture 11: Lewis structures, domains and molecular shapes]{ref}

Three worked counts have the same total of eight <valence electrons>. In <methane>, <carbon>'s four plus the contributions of four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> give $4+4=8$: four <bond pairs>, no <lone pairs>, and <tetrahedral molecular geometry>. In <ammonia>, <nitrogen>'s five plus three from <hydrogen> give $5+3=8$: three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>, producing <trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry>. In <water>, <oxygen>'s six plus two from <hydrogen> give $6+2=8$: two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>, producing <bent molecular geometry>. All three have four <electron domains>; their <molecular geometries> differ.

\Image[vsepr-four-domain-shapes.png]
{title=Four electron domains give three molecular shapes as lone pairs replace bonded neighbours}

The regular tetrahedral directions can be represented by the <unit vectors>
$$
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,1,1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,-1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,-1,1).
$$
Each pair has <dot product> $-1/3$, so the ideal <bond angle> is $\theta=\arccos(-1/3)\approx109.47^\circ$. This is a geometric calculation, rather than a prediction that every four-domain <molecule> has that exact <bond angle>. <Lone pairs> and different kinds of <chemical bonds> distort the ideal arrangement; <water>'s H–O–H angle is about $104.5^\circ$.\a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity][Chemistry 2e, 7.6: ideal geometry and water's bond angle]{ref}

The model is attractive because small-number bookkeeping produces a spatial prediction without first solving for every <molecular orbital>. Shape then helps explain <chemical polarity> and which <hydrogen bond donor> or <hydrogen bond acceptor> sites are accessible. It is a qualitative model, not a full calculation of the electronic <energy> or a universal solution for every <chemical compound>. Gillespie and Nyholm's 1957 review \a[https://doi.org/10.1039/QR9571100339][Inorganic stereochemistry, Quarterly Reviews 11, 339–380]{ref} is a historical anchor for its development.

= Electron domain
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

An <electron domain> is a region of <electron density> counted around a central <chemical atom> in the <VSEPR model>. One <lone pair> counts as one domain; a bond to one neighbour counts as one domain even when its <bond order> is two or three. Domain count is therefore different from simply counting <electron pairs>.

= Electron pair geometry
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

<Electron pair geometry> describes the arrangement of all <electron domains> in the <VSEPR model>, including <lone pairs>. <Molecular geometry> instead describes the arrangement of <chemical atoms>. Four domains have a tetrahedral ideal arrangement, but keeping only the bonded neighbours gives the different <molecular geometries> of <methane>, <ammonia> and <water>.

= Bond pair
{parent=Lewis structure}

= Bonding pair
{synonym}

A <bond pair> is an <electron pair> allocated to a connection between two <chemical atoms> in a <Lewis structure>. A single line stands for one <bond pair>, or two <electrons>. A double connection contains two <bond pairs>, or four <electrons>; a triple connection contains three <bond pairs>, or six <electrons>. A <bond pair> is shared bookkeeping for the combined arrangement, rather than two distinguishable <electrons> each permanently owned by one endpoint.

In <water>, two <bond pairs> use $2\times2=4$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In <ammonia>, three <bond pairs> use $3\times2=6$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each C=O connection contains two <bond pairs>, so there are four <bond pairs> in the entire <carbon dioxide> <molecule>. Counting connections and counting <bond pairs> therefore give different answers for multiple connections.

The same <bond pair> is treated differently for different questions: both endpoints count its two <electrons> for the <octet rule>, whereas each endpoint receives just one <electron> for <formal charge>. Neither convention asserts equal physical <electron density> at the endpoints. For electronic shapes, occupancy, and <energy>, see <molecular orbital theory> rather than treating a <bond pair> line as an entire <wavefunction>.

= Lone pair
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

A <lone pair> is an <electron pair> assigned to one <chemical atom> without a connecting line to another <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure>. It is usually shown as two dots. <Water> has two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>, accounting for four <valence electrons>; <ammonia> has one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>, accounting for two. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>, and <carbon> has none.

A <lone pair> can be reactive. In <protonation> of <ammonia>, the <nitrogen> <lone pair> becomes a new <bond pair> to a <proton>:
$$
\mathrm{NH}_3+\mathrm H^+\longrightarrow\mathrm{NH}_4^+.
$$
The <proton> supplies no <electron>, so the eight <valence electrons> are retained: three <bond pairs> plus one <lone pair> become four <bond pairs>. The resulting <nitrogen> has <formal charge> $5-0-8/2=+1$; each <hydrogen> has <formal charge> zero. This is why identifying an available <lone pair> helps explain the <chemical base> behaviour of <ammonia>, rather than merely decorating its formula.

Lewis already used the <ammonia> to $\mathrm{NH}_4^+$ example in \a[https://zenodo.org/records/1429068][The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 777–778]{ref}. In modern terms the dots identify an allocation of <electrons>, not two stationary particles or an isolated patch unaffected by the rest of the <molecule>. A <molecular orbital> description can spread electronic amplitude over several <chemical atoms> while a localized <Lewis structure> remains useful for <acid-base chemistry>.

= Formal charge
{parent=Lewis structure}
{title2=$\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$}
{wiki}

The <formal charge> of a <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure> assigns it all of its nonbonding <electrons> and half of the <electrons> in its connections:
$$
\mathrm{FC}=v-n-\frac b2.
$$
Here $v$ is the neutral <chemical atom>'s <valence electron> count, $n$ is the number of nonbonding <electrons> assigned to it, and $b$ is the number of bonding <electrons> incident on it. Count <electrons>, not <electron pairs>: two <lone pairs> mean $n=4$, and two double connections mean $b=8$. The number $\mathrm{FC}$ gives <electric charge> in units of the positive <elementary charge>. Equal splitting is a bookkeeping convention, regardless of the actual <electron density>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08169][IUPAC Gold Book, formal charge]{ref}.

\Image[lewis-carbon-dioxide-formal-charge.png]
{title=Sixteen <valence electrons> in two <carbon dioxide> <Lewis structures>: $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ has zero <formal charges>, while $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ has separated <formal charges>}
{description=The first <Lewis structure> has two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. The second has three <lone pairs> on the singly connected <oxygen>, one on the triply connected <oxygen>, and none on <carbon>. Both use four <bond pairs> and four <lone pairs>; both satisfy the <octet rule>. Their contributions to <chemical resonance> are unequal, and the drawn <formal charges> are not <partial atomic charges>.}

For $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has $v=6$, $n=4$, $b=4$, giving <formal charge> $6-4-4/2=0$. <Carbon> has $v=4$, $n=0$, $b=8$, giving <formal charge> $4-0-8/2=0$. For $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$, the left <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-6-2/2=-1$, <carbon> still has <formal charge> zero, and the right <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-2-6/2=+1$. The net <electric charge> is zero in either drawing.

The <Lewis structure> with no separated <formal charges> is the dominant contributor in this elementary comparison; the charge-separated drawing is a less important contributor, rather than an equally dominant alternative. This is a qualitative selection rule, not a calculation of <energy> or numerical <chemical resonance> weights. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: comparing carbon dioxide structures]{ref}. A zero <formal charge> does not imply zero <partial atomic charge>: the latter depends on partitioning the actual <electron density>, which a <Lewis structure> does not supply.

As a short consistency derivation, summing $n+b/2$ over all <chemical atoms> counts every drawn <electron> once: a nonbonding <electron> is assigned once, and a bonding <electron> contributes half at each of its two endpoints. Thus
$$
\sum_i\mathrm{FC}_i=\sum_i v_i-N_{\mathrm{valence}},
$$
where $N_{\mathrm{valence}}$ is the total drawn <valence electron> count. The right side is the net <electric charge> in units of <elementary charge>. This catches a wrong budget even when individual <octet rule> checks look plausible.

= Resonance
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

= Chemical resonance
{synonym}

A single <nitrate> <Lewis structure> singles out one N=O connection, yet the three N–O <chemical bonds> of the isolated symmetric <ion> are equivalent. <Chemical resonance> reconciles that symmetry with the integer <electron pair> allocations of a <Lewis structure>. The equal <chemical bond> lengths in <nitrate> are discussed in \a[https://www.open.edu/openlearn/mod/oucontent/view.php?id=87848&section=_unit5.2][The Open University, Discovering chemistry: resonance structures]{ref}.

<Chemical resonance> uses several <Lewis structures> with the same <chemical atom> connections and total <electric charge> to describe electronic structure that one allocation cannot fully express. The <chemical atoms> stay in place; the positions of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ between drawings. A two-headed arrow $\leftrightarrow$ connects the drawings and is distinct from a <chemical equilibrium> arrow $\rightleftharpoons$. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08205][IUPAC Gold Book, resonance form]{ref}.

In each <nitrate> <resonance structure>, use the existing <formal charge> convention $\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FC}(\mathrm N)&=5-0-8/2=+1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{singly connected O})&=6-6-2/2=-1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{doubly connected O})&=6-4-4/2=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $v$ counts neutral-<chemical atom> <valence electrons>, $n$ counts nonbonding <electrons>, and $b$ counts incident bonding <electrons>. The sum is $+1-1-1+0=-1$, agreeing with the <nitrate ion>'s net <electric charge>. The <formal charge> rules and their distinction from actual atomic <electric charge> are given in \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: formal charges and resonance]{ref}.

Keep one labelled <oxygen> fixed while reading the three <nitrate> <resonance structures>. Its connection is double in one drawing and single in two. Equal weighting within this three-contributor <Lewis structure> model gives the average <bond order>
$$
\overline b_{\mathrm{N-O}}=\frac{2+1+1}{3}=\frac43.
$$
The same calculation applies to all three N–O connections. Averaging the drawn <formal charges> similarly gives $-2/3$ per <oxygen>, while <nitrogen> retains $+1$; $+1+3(-2/3)=-1$. These fractions summarize the chosen contributors. They are not a measurement of <partial atomic charge>, a literal fraction of an <electron> sitting on an <oxygen>, or time spent in one drawing.

\Image[nitrate-delocalized-bonding.png]
{title=Equivalent N–O <chemical bonds> in symmetric <nitrate>: the equal-contributor <bond order> count is $4/3$}
{description=All three connections have the same graphical treatment. A solid line with a dashed companion is a schematic indication of shared multiple-<chemical bond> character, not a plot of <electron density> or a physical fractional line. The labels $4/3$ record an average within three octet <Lewis structures>; no universal, directly measured <bond order> of exactly $1.333\ldots$ is asserted.}

The <resonance hybrid> has one spatially continuous <electron density> extending over the <ion>. None of its N–O <chemical bonds> is permanently selected as the double connection of one contributor. The dashed marks above are symbols; <electrons> do not travel along them or make the marks float between <chemical atoms>. For isolated symmetric <nitrate>, symmetry makes the three N–O <chemical bonds> equivalent; an environment that distinguishes the <oxygen> sites can remove that equivalence. The fraction $4/3$ alone predicts neither a <chemical bond> length nor a dissociation <energy>. The general relation between multiple <chemical bonds>, lengths, and strengths is discussed in \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-5-strengths-of-ionic-and-covalent-bonds][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.5: strengths of ionic and covalent bonds]{ref}; its tabulated averages are not a nitrate-specific calibration of $4/3$.

<Ozone> provides a useful two-contributor comparison. Its $3\times6=18$ <valence electrons> give three <bond pairs> and six <lone pairs>. One <resonance structure> has a double O–O connection on the left and a single one on the right; the other reverses these allocations while retaining the same <chemical atom> positions and connections. The central <oxygen> has one <lone pair> and <formal charge> $+1$; the singly connected terminal <oxygen> has three <lone pairs> and <formal charge> $-1$; the doubly connected terminal <oxygen> has two <lone pairs> and <formal charge> zero. Thus $6+12=18$ <electrons> and zero net <electric charge> are conserved. The equal-contributor <bond order> count is $(2+1)/2=3/2$ for either connection. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/3549685d450773a066a716832634203f_MIT5_111F14_Lec10.pdf][MIT 5.111, lecture 10, pp. 4–5: ozone resonance structures]{ref}.

For <carbon dioxide>, $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ and $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ share sixteen <valence electrons> and the same O–C–O connections. The mirror drawing $\mathrm{O^+\equiv C{-}O^-}$ is also possible. The two charge-separated drawings are equivalent to one another for an isolated symmetric <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, but neither is equally dominant with $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ in the elementary <formal charge> comparison.

<Chemical resonance> does not mean that a <molecule> rapidly flips between drawings, nor that separate <molecules> at <chemical equilibrium> carry the individual drawings. The actual <electron density> belongs to one combined electronic description. In a <quantum mechanics> treatment, contributors enter a <linear combination> of many-<electron> <wavefunctions>; a <molecular orbital> treatment can describe the extended electronic arrangement through <molecular orbitals>. The drawings alone provide neither a measured density nor numerical weights. Their usefulness is to expose the limitations of one localized allocation without pretending that every algebraically allowed <Lewis structure> contributes equally.

= Resonance structure
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

= Resonance form
{synonym}

= Resonance contributor
{synonym}

A <resonance structure> is one <Lewis structure> contributing to a <chemical resonance> description. The contributors retain the same <atomic nuclei> in the same positions, the same connections, and the same total <valence electron> count and <electric charge>; allocations of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ. They are alternative representations of one electronic structure, not distinct substances with different atomic arrangements. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance][Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance forms]{ref}.

= Resonance hybrid
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

A <resonance hybrid> is the combined electronic description expressed using several <resonance structures>. The word does not describe a mixture of separate <molecules> or <ions>: a <nitrate ion> has one <electron density>, rather than three species in <chemical equilibrium>. Equal weighting is appropriate for the equivalent <nitrate> contributors in the elementary model; the unequal <carbon dioxide> contributors illustrate why it is not a general rule. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance][Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance hybrids do not fluctuate between forms]{ref}.

= Octet rule
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

The <octet rule> is a useful empirical guide: many <Lewis structures> of <chemical compounds> containing <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> give each of those <chemical atoms> eight <electrons> when nonbonding <electrons> and all incident bonding <electrons> are counted. <Hydrogen> instead normally counts two <electrons>. These are local counts, not independent budgets: a shared <bond pair> counts at both endpoints but only once in the <molecule>'s <valence electron> total.

In <water>, <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$ <electrons>; in <ammonia>, <nitrogen> counts $2+6=8$; in $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, <carbon> counts $0+8=8$ and each <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$. The charge-separated <carbon dioxide> drawing also satisfies the <octet rule>, so the <octet rule> alone cannot select the dominant contributor. <Formal charge> provides another check.

The <octet rule> is a heuristic rather than a theorem about every <chemical bond>. Odd <valence electron> totals cannot be partitioned entirely into <electron pairs>, and some valid <Lewis structures> have fewer or more than eight <electrons> around a <chemical atom>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures][Chemistry 2e, 7.3: exceptions to the octet rule]{ref}. The capacity of the $s$ and $p$ <electron subshells>, $2+6=8$, helps motivate the guide for these <chemical elements>, but does not prove universal compliance. Lewis discussed a stable outer group of eight, together with a two-<electron> arrangement for <hydrogen>, in \a[https://zenodo.org/records/1429068][The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 768 and 774]{ref}.

= Valence electron
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <valence electron> belongs to the electronic arrangement used to describe a <chemical atom>'s <chemical bonds> and ordinary <chemical reactions>. For the neutral <hydrogen>, <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> <chemical atoms> used here, the <valence electrons> occupy the outermost occupied <electron shell>. Their respective <electron configurations> are $1s^1$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^2$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^3$, and $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^4$, giving one, four, five, and six <valence electrons>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations][Chemistry 2e, 6.4: electron configurations and valence electrons]{ref}.

<Lewis structures> show these <valence electrons> while omitting the inner <electrons>. Consequently, eight drawn <valence electrons> in <water> do not mean eight total <electrons>: neutral <water> has ten, with two inner <oxygen> <electrons> omitted. Neutral <ammonia> likewise has ten total <electrons> but eight <valence electrons>; neutral <carbon dioxide> has twenty-two total <electrons> but sixteen <valence electrons>.

This distinction makes the small counts useful: connections and possible <lone pairs> can be organized without representing every <electron> or solving the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. The outermost-<electron shell> shortcut applies to the examples above, rather than serving as a universal definition for all <chemical elements>; the electronic arrangement relevant to <chemical bonds> can also involve other <electron subshells>.

= Polymer
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <polymer> is a large molecular chain or network built from many chemically linked repeating units. Statistical chain models idealize its microscopic structure by segment vectors and describe its conformation through distributions, correlations and scattering. A <freely jointed chain> fixes each segment length while allowing independent orientations; a <Gaussian chain> replaces the segment or coarse-grained displacement distribution by a <Gaussian distribution>. These ideal models neglect excluded-volume and other segment interactions.

= Flory approximation
{c}
{parent=Polymer}
{title2=$\mathcal F/(k_BT)\sim R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$}

The <Flory approximation> balances the entropic cost of expanding an <ideal chain> against a mean-field <excluded volume> interaction. For $N$ segments of length $b$ in $d$ spatial dimensions, minimizing $R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$ gives $R^{d+2}\sim vN^3b^2$. This yields the scaling exponents $3/5$ in three dimensions and $3/4$ in two dimensions. It is a scaling approximation, not an exact numerical free energy or proof of critical exponents; confinement replaces $R^d$ by the accessible coil volume.

= Polymerization
{parent=Polymer}

<Polymerization> joins molecular units into a <polymer>. For a filament end exchanging units with a solution, addition and removal rates determine growth, while steric constraints and an applied force can modify those rates.

= Monomer
{parent=Polymer}

A <monomer> is a molecular unit that can join other units through <polymerization>. A statistical link in a coarse-grained chain model need not coincide with one chemical monomer.

= Nucleic acid
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid> is a <polymer> built from linked <nucleotides>. The two principal kinds are <DNA> and <RNA>. Each <nucleotide> contributes a <sugar>, a <nucleobase>, and <phosphate> to the chain; the <nucleobase> is only the letter-bearing component, not the entire <nucleotide>. <DNA> uses <deoxyribose>, whereas <RNA> uses <ribose>. A chain of six <nucleotides> is therefore not six free <nucleobases> placed beside one another. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleic-Acids][NHGRI: nucleic acids]{ref}.

The chemical scaffold and the variable lettering do different jobs. <Covalent bonds> maintain the <sugar>–<phosphate> backbone, while the order of attached <nucleobases> specifies the <nucleic acid sequence>. A <phosphodiester bond> links the $3'$ position of one <sugar> to the $5'$ position of the next through <phosphate>. These position numbers refer to <carbon> <chemical atoms> in the <sugar>; they define a strand's direction even when it bends or forms a circle. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/][Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition: the human genome]{ref}.

This division is useful: the backbone supplies a connected physical record, and <base pairing> allows its letters to recognize partners. <DNA replication> copies that record, and <transcription> uses a <DNA> template to make <RNA>. <RNA> can also fold into functional shapes through <base pairing> within one chain; a <nucleic acid sequence> specifies chemical order, not a requirement for a straight shape. The beauty is that the same kind of <polymer> can carry a message and form a structure that acts on messages.

= Nucleic acid sequence
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Nucleotide sequence
{synonym}

A <nucleic acid sequence> records the ordered <nucleobases> of a <nucleic acid> strand, conventionally read from $5'$ to $3'$. The standard <DNA> alphabet is <adenine> A, <cytosine> C, <guanine> G, and <thymine> T; the standard <RNA> alphabet uses <uracil> U in place of <thymine> T. Each letter denotes the <nucleobase> attached to one <nucleotide>, so the six-letter <DNA> <nucleic acid sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$ describes six linked <nucleotides>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair][NHGRI: representing DNA by a sequence of letters]{ref}.

Order carries information that composition loses. Both ACGTAC and AACCGT contain two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>, but their <nucleic acid sequences> differ at positions 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. At position 2, for example, the first has <cytosine> and the second <adenine>. Reporting only the four counts cannot reconstruct either ordered record.

To obtain the partner of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$, apply the <DNA> <base pairing> rules position by position: A selects T, C selects G, G selects C, T selects A, A selects T, and C selects G. Alignment gives $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TGCATG}\text{-}5'$. Its opposite end labels matter because the two are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. Reading the partner from its own $5'$ end reverses the aligned lettering and yields the <reverse complement>, $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GTACGT}\text{-}3'$.

\Image[dna-reverse-complement.png]
{title=Complement, then reverse: <DNA> ACGTAC has aligned partner TGCATG and <reverse complement> GTACGT}

Reversal alone gives CATGCA; complementation alone gives aligned TGCATG. Neither operation alone gives the partner written in the usual $5'$-to-$3'$ convention. Applying the <reverse complement> operation twice returns ACGTAC: GTACGT complements to CATGCA, which reverses to ACGTAC. This convention makes a single reported strand sufficient to specify an ideally complementary <DNA> duplex, provided its direction is known.

= Reverse complement
{parent=Nucleic acid sequence}

A <nucleotide sequence> obtained by reversing the order and complementing each <nucleobase> according to the appropriate <base pairing> rules.

= Base pair
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=bp}

A <base pair> is an association of two <nucleobases> through <hydrogen bonds> in a compatible arrangement. It commonly joins opposite strands of a <nucleic acid double helix>, but can also join two regions of one folded <RNA> strand. Canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs> match <adenine> with <thymine> and <guanine> with <cytosine>; canonical <RNA> <base pairs> use <adenine> with <uracil> instead. The <nucleobases> belong to complete <nucleotides>, including their attached <sugars> and backbone <phosphate>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair][NHGRI: base pair]{ref}.

The abbreviation bp also measures the length of a paired region. Our ACGTAC/TGCATG duplex has six <base pairs>, containing twelve <nucleotides> altogether. Its upper strand contains two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>; the lower contains one, one, two, and two respectively. Combined, the counts are three of each <nucleobase>. Equality of total <adenine> and <thymine>, or of total <guanine> and <cytosine>, need not hold within either individual strand.

= Base pairing
{parent=Base pair}

<Base pairing> is the recognition and association of compatible <nucleobases>. In canonical Watson–Crick geometry, an <adenine>–<thymine> <base pair> has two <hydrogen bonds>, while a <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pair> has three. These counts refer to those specific geometries and usual chemical forms; they are not a definition of every possible <base pair>. <RNA> can also contain other pairings, and canonical <adenine>–<uracil> pairing replaces <adenine>–<thymine> pairing. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-2-dna-structure-and-sequencing][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: DNA structure]{ref}.

\Image[dna-antiparallel-base-pairs.png]
{title=Six canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs>: opposite backbone directions and two or three <hydrogen bonds> per pair}

For this illustrative duplex, the six <base pairs> are A–T, C–G, G–C, T–A, A–T, and C–G. Thus three contribute two <hydrogen bonds> each and three contribute three each:
$$
3\times2+3\times3=15.
$$
Each dotted connector in the diagram counts one <hydrogen bond>; coloured boxes denote whole <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. The solid rails denote the <covalent bond> scaffold. Separating the two strands disrupts <base pairing> and <base stacking> without requiring the backbone to be cut.

The count of fifteen <hydrogen bonds> is not fifteen identical contributions to a duplex's <Gibbs free energy>. <Base stacking>, interactions with <water> and <ions>, <temperature>, and the order of neighbouring <base pairs> also matter. Knowing the fraction of <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pairs> alone does not fix a <nucleic acid double helix>'s shape or stability. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/][Brown, Genomes: pairing and stacking interactions]{ref}. What makes <base pairing> especially useful is its selectivity: an exposed <DNA> strand constrains the partner's <nucleic acid sequence>, giving <DNA replication> a chemical recognition rule.

= Nucleic acid double helix
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid double helix> consists of two <nucleic acid> strands winding around a common axis. In the familiar <DNA> arrangement, <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones lie outside and paired <nucleobases> lie inside, stacked along the axis. The strands are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. The flattened <base pairing> ladder above displays partners and direction; winding that ladder into a <helix> adds spatial organization. <RNA> can also form double-helical paired regions, so the geometry is not exclusive to <DNA>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: DNA structure]{ref}.

The useful surprise is that a structure explains a copying principle. Complementary <base pairs> allow each <DNA> strand to specify the other's <nucleic acid sequence>; separate strands can each act as templates in <DNA replication>. This is a structural constraint, while the <enzymes> and stages of <DNA replication> supply the actual process. A <nucleic acid sequence> specifies letter order without, by itself, giving every detail of the three-dimensional <helix>.

On 25 April 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick published a model with two oppositely directed chains, outside <phosphate> groups, and specific <base pairs>. Their short paper explicitly connected this pairing to a possible copying mechanism, while describing the model as requiring further experimental tests. See the original \a[https://doi.org/10.1038/171737a0][Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids: A Structure for Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid, Nature 171, 737–738]{ref}, with an accessible \a[https://inters.org/files/watsoncrick1953.pdf][reprint of the original paper]{ref}.

Rosalind Franklin and Raymond Gosling's paper in the same issue had a different role: it presented <X-ray> <diffraction> evidence from <DNA> fibres and its structural interpretation, including the helical character of the wetter B form and evidence for <phosphate> groups on the outside. They distinguished forms produced at different water contents and assessed compatibility with the Watson–Crick model. Read their original \a[https://doi.org/10.1038/171740a0][Molecular Configuration in Sodium Thymonucleate, Nature 171, 740–741 (1953)]{ref}. The model and the experimental analysis are distinct contributions; a <diffraction> pattern is evidence constraining a <molecular geometry>, rather than a direct picture of letter-by-letter <base pairing>.

= Antiparallel nucleic acid strands
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

Two <nucleic acid> strands with opposite directional alignment: a $5'$-to-$3'$ direction on one is aligned with the $3'$-to-$5'$ direction on the other.

= Base stacking
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

<Base stacking> is the close arrangement and interaction of neighbouring <nucleobases> with their approximately planar faces above one another. Along a <nucleic acid double helix>, neighbouring <base pairs> stack successively; <base pairing> instead associates partners across the two strands. Both affect duplex stability, and the interactions depend on the identities and arrangement of neighbouring <nucleobases>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/][Brown, Genomes: base pairing and base stacking]{ref}.

= Stem-loop
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Deoxyribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= DNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Deoxyribonucleic acid>, or <DNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> that stores biological information in its <nucleotide sequence>. The crucial variable is order: two <DNA> <molecules> can contain the same numbers of each <nucleobase> yet carry different <nucleotide sequences>. This makes a physical <molecule> an inheritable record, rather than merely a mixture of ingredients. <Genes> are functional regions of that record; the whole <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> is not one long <gene>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Deoxyribonucleic-Acid-Fact-Sheet][NHGRI: DNA fact sheet]{ref}.

Each <DNA> strand has a backbone of alternating <deoxyribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups joined through <covalent bonds>. Attached <nucleobases> supply the familiar four-letter alphabet: <adenine> A, <thymine> T, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. In ordinary double-stranded <DNA>, <base pairing> matches A with T and G with C. The backbone connects successive <nucleotides> along a strand; <base pairing> connects partners across strands. Keeping these two connections distinct explains how strands can separate while each retains its own <nucleotide sequence>.

The two backbones have directions, conventionally labelled $5'$ and $3'$ after numbered <carbon> <chemical atoms> in <deoxyribose>. They form <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: following one from $5'$ to $3'$ takes you opposite to the other's $5'$-to-$3'$ direction. For the chosen <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$, write its partner directly underneath as $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. Now read that partner from its own $5'$ end: its <reverse complement> is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GGCAT}\text{-}3'$. Complementing letters and reversing their order are separate operations. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the structure and function of DNA]{ref}.

\Image[dna-antiparallel-complement.png]
{title=<DNA> <base pairing> and <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: an unrolled ladder for ATGCC and its <reverse complement>}

The diagram's rails represent <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones and its coloured blocks represent <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. Reading left to right gives five matched positions: A–T, T–A, G–C, C–G, C–G. There are two A–T-type <base pairs> and three G–C-type <base pairs>; the complete two-strand example contains ten <nucleotides>. Reversing the bottom row changes the reading convention, not the physical partners. Real double-stranded <DNA> winds into a <nucleic acid double helix>; the flattened ladder emphasizes <base pairing> and direction without pretending to show its three-dimensional geometry.

The motivating consequence is copying by a template. After strands separate, each existing <DNA> strand specifies which incoming <nucleotides> should accompany it through <base pairing>. <DNA replication> uses <enzymes> to build complementary strands, allowing the record to persist through successive <biological cells>. <Base pairing> supplies a selection rule, while <enzymes> perform the chemistry: a matching diagram alone is not a complete copying mechanism. The connection between structure and copying was already central to the model published by Watson and Crick on 25 April 1953. See \a[https://www.nature.com/articles/171737a0][Watson and Crick, Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids, Nature 171, 737–738 (1953)]{ref}.

Stored information needs interpretation. Some <DNA> regions specify <proteins> through <RNA>; others contribute to functional <RNA> or regulate <gene expression>, and some have no known function. <Non-coding DNA> means sequence that does not specify a <protein>'s sequence of <amino acids>, not automatically useless sequence. Nor does a <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> alone determine an <organism>'s fate: <phenotypes> also depend on biological context and environment. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Non-Coding-DNA][NHGRI: non-coding DNA]{ref}. Its double-stranded form is often modeled mechanically as a <worm-like chain>, with <elasticity> and effective <excluded volume> depending on solvent conditions.

= Ribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= RNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Ribonucleic acid>, or <RNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> whose <nucleotide sequence> can both carry information and organize a working molecular shape. Its backbone contains alternating <ribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups connected through <covalent bonds>. Its usual <nucleobases> are <adenine> A, <uracil> U, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. Thus <RNA> uses U where <DNA> uses T; its <ribose> also differs chemically from <DNA>'s <deoxyribose>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Ribonucleic-Acid-RNA][NHGRI: RNA]{ref}.

In <transcription (biology)>, an <enzyme> uses one <DNA> strand as a template for <RNA>. For example, the <DNA> template $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$ specifies $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGCC}\text{-}3'$ in <RNA>. The <base pairing> rules now include A–U rather than A–T, while G–C remains. This <RNA> matches the other <DNA> strand's $5'$-to-$3'$ lettering after T is replaced by U. Identifying the template strand matters: replacing T by U in an arbitrarily chosen <DNA> strand is not a general method for deriving the <RNA> product. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: from DNA to RNA]{ref}.

<RNA> is often a single strand, but single-stranded does not mean straight or entirely unpaired. Complementary regions within one <RNA> <molecule> can undergo <base pairing>, bringing distant positions together. Consider the illustrative twelve-<nucleotide> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GCAUUUUUAUGC}\text{-}3'$. Pair positions 1 with 12, 2 with 11, 3 with 10, and 4 with 9. The resulting <stem-loop> has four <base pairs>: G–C, C–G, A–U, U–A. Positions 5–8 remain unpaired in the loop. Both arms belong to the same continuous backbone, and the paired arms have the directions of <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>.

\Image[rna-sequence-and-folding.png]
{title=One <RNA> <nucleotide sequence>, two drawings: numbered letters and an illustrative <stem-loop> with four <base pairs>}

The diagram proposes a possible pairing pattern, not a prediction that this short <RNA> must adopt that shape under every condition. Actual <RNA> folding depends on additional interactions, <temperature>, <ions>, and associated <proteins>; the full three-dimensional arrangement contains more information than the drawn <base pairs>. The example's purpose is to show how a linear <nucleotide sequence> can specify contacts within itself.

Different <RNA> roles make this flexibility useful. <Messenger RNA> carries a message that a <ribosome> reads during <translation (biology)> to build a <protein>. <Transfer RNA> helps match that message to <amino acid> building blocks; see \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transfer-RNA-tRNA][NHGRI: transfer RNA]{ref}. <Ribosomal RNA> forms structural and catalytic parts of the <ribosome>. Other <RNA> participates in <gene regulatory networks>, changing how strongly <genes> are expressed. Many <RNA> <molecules> therefore function without being translated into <proteins>; for some <genes>, functional <RNA> is the product itself.

Most strikingly, some folded <RNA> acts as an <enzyme>: these catalytic <RNA> <molecules> are <ribozymes>. The <ribosome>'s bond-forming <active site> is built from <RNA>, although the complete <ribosome> also contains <proteins>. Structural evidence appears in \a[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/][Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)]{ref}. <RNA> therefore connects a readable record with physical recognition, regulation, and catalysis: the same kind of <polymer> can be a message, an adaptor, or part of molecular machinery.

= Messenger RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= mRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Transfer RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= tRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Ribosomal RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= rRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Radius of gyration
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}
{title2=$R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle$}

For $N$ equally weighted positions, the squared <radius of gyration> is the average squared distance from the <center of mass>: $R_g^2=N^{-1}\sum_n\langle|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2\rangle$, where $\mathbf R_{\rm cm}=N^{-1}\sum_n\mathbf R_n$. Expanding pair differences gives $\sum_{m,n}|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2=2N\sum_n|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2$, and therefore
$$
R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle.
$$
For a <Gaussian chain> or <freely jointed chain> with these $N$ positions and mean squared link length $b^2$, each pair <variance> is $b^2|m-n|$. Summing separations yields $R_g^2=b^2(N^2-1)/(6N)\sim Nb^2/6$. Counting all endpoints of $N$ links instead gives $N+1$ positions and the corresponding finite-size formula.

= Ion
{parent=Chemistry}
{title2=$q=ze$}

An <ion> is a <chemical atom> or a group of <chemical atoms> with a nonzero net <electric charge>. A positive <ion> is a <cation>; a negative <ion> is an <anion>. The whole entity carries the <electric charge>: a <hydronium> <ion> is a group of four <chemical atoms> with net charge $+e$. This definition agrees with \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03158][IUPAC Gold Book: ion]{ref}.

For an ordinary <ion> containing only <atomic nuclei> and <electrons>, let $N_p$ count all its <protons> and $N_e$ all its <electrons>. Each <proton> contributes the <elementary charge> $+e$, each <electron> contributes $-e$, and <neutrons> contribute zero. Therefore
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e=ze,\qquad z=N_p-N_e.
$$
The signed integer $z$ is the net <electric charge> number. Removing an <electron> increases $z$ by one; adding an <electron> decreases $z$ by one. Changing the <electron> count leaves the <atomic number>, and therefore the identity of the <chemical element>, unchanged.

For example, a neutral <sodium> <chemical atom> has $11$ <protons> and $11$ <electrons>. Its <cation> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ still has $11$ <protons>, but only $10$ <electrons>, so $q=(11-10)e=+e$. A neutral <chlorine> <chemical atom> has $17$ <protons> and $17$ <electrons>; its <anion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ has $17$ <protons> and $18$ <electrons>, so $q=(17-18)e=-e$. These are particle inventories, not pictures of <atomic orbitals>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-6-ionic-and-molecular-compounds][Flowers, Theopold, Langley and Robinson, Chemistry 2e, §2.6: ions and ionic compounds]{ref}.

\Image[ion-charge-bookkeeping.png]
{title=<Ion> <electric charge> bookkeeping: neutral <sodium> and <chlorine> <chemical atoms> compared with their <cation> and <anion>}
{description=Every colored dot counts one <proton> or one <electron>. Rows are counting arrays, not spatial models of <atomic nuclei> or <electron shells>. The net <electric charges> are $0,+e,0,-e$.}

One <sodium chloride> <formula unit> accounts for one $\mathrm{Na}^+$ <cation> and one $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <anion>, whose net <electric charges> sum to zero. Ordinary solid <sodium chloride> is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. This is a useful distinction: a neutral material can contain charged constituents, and its <chemical formula> tells us their ratio without claiming that each pair is a separate <molecule>.

<Ion> bookkeeping connects <chemistry> to <electric current>: moving <ions> can transport <electric charge>, while stationary <ions> cannot supply a transport <current density>. In <electrochemistry>, both signs of <ions> can contribute to the same <conventional current>. An <ion> opposing a fixed <electric charge> is called a <counterion>.

= Cation
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

A <cation> is an <ion> with positive net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Na}^+$ or <hydronium>.

= Anion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

An <anion> is an <ion> with negative net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ or <hydroxide>.

= Nitrate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO}_3^-$}
{wiki}

= Nitrate ion
{synonym}

The <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, is an <anion> with one <nitrogen> connected to three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> and net <electric charge> $-1$ in units of <elementary charge>. Its 24 <valence electrons> admit three equivalent octet <resonance structures>; see <Lewis structure> for their construction and <chemical resonance> for their interpretation. The N–O connections are <covalent bonds> within the <ion>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-2-covalent-bonding][Chemistry 2e, 7.2: bonding within nitrate and between ions]{ref}.

= Hydrogen ion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H^+$}

In <acid-base chemistry>, <hydrogen ion> usually refers to positively charged hydrogen, represented by $\mathrm H^+$. In <water>, this notation describes solvated species, commonly represented by <hydronium>, rather than an isolated bare <proton>. The <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> defines <pH>.

= Hydronium
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+$}

<Hydronium> is the <ion> formed when a <water> <molecule> accepts a <proton>. It provides the usual aqueous shorthand for the <hydrogen ion> used in <pH> calculations.

= Hydroxide
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}^-$}

= Counterion
{parent=Ion}

A mobile <ion> whose charge has the opposite sign to a charged macromolecule, surface or other fixed species. Its local <concentration> can increase close to that fixed charge; <counterion condensation> distinguishes a bound fraction that remains localized in an infinite-dilution limit.

= Electrochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Electrochemistry> studies how <chemical reactions>, transport, and <chemical equilibrium> couple to <electric charge> and <electric potential>. In a cell, <electrons> travel through the external circuit, while mobile <ions> carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte>. At the <electrodes>, <redox> reactions connect these two forms of <electric charge> transport. This connection makes it possible both to obtain electrical <work> from a <chemical reaction> and to use electrical <work> to drive a <chemical reaction>.

An <anode> is an <electrode> where <oxidation> occurs; a <cathode> is an <electrode> where <reduction> occurs. These definitions refer to the <chemical reactions>, rather than universal positive or negative terminal signs. In a <galvanic cell> delivering electrical <work>, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive. In an <electrolytic cell> driven by an external source, the <anode> is positive and the <cathode> negative. See \a[https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109][Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), §§3.1 and 3.4]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.2: galvanic cells]{ref}.

For a worked example, consider an <electrolytic cell> containing molten <sodium chloride>, with the <anode> connected to the positive terminal and the <cathode> to the negative terminal of an external source. At the <cathode>, <sodium> <cations> gain <electrons>; at the <anode>, <chlorine> <anions> lose <electrons>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2e^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na},\\
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}_2+2e^-,\\
\text{overall:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na}+\mathrm{Cl}_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first line has net <electric charge> zero on each side; the second has $-2e$ on each side. Adding the lines cancels the transferred <electrons> and preserves both <chemical atom> counts and net <electric charge>. The <sodium> product is neutral, and the <chlorine> product is a diatomic <molecule>. These equations specify molten <sodium chloride>; aqueous <chemical solutions> introduce competing <water> reactions. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-7-electrolysis][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.7: electrolysis of molten sodium chloride]{ref}.

<Electrochemistry> underlies <battery> charging and the manufacture of chemicals by <electrolysis>. Its particularly useful idea is that an <electron> inventory in a balanced <chemical equation> also accounts for transported <electric charge>. Transport alone is a different question from an <electrode> <chemical reaction>: an <ion> may carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte> without itself being consumed at an <electrode>.

Historically, Michael Faraday introduced the terms <electrode>, <electrolyte>, <anion>, <cation>, and <ion> in the terminology section of his 1834 paper, \a[https://knowledge.electrochem.org/estir/hist/hist-06-Faraday-7.pdf][Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series, §§662–667]{ref}. His §667 explicitly treats the direction assigned to <electric current> as a convention. His <ion> language predates the modern <electron> and <atomic nucleus> picture used in the charge-counting example above.

For an <electrolyte> near <chemical equilibrium>, a <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> can approximate the distribution of <ions> in an <electric potential>. Such an equilibrium model does not by itself describe a cell carrying sustained <electric current>.

= Electrode
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

An <electrode> connects an electronic <electrical conductor> to an <electrolyte>. In a cell carrying sustained <redox> <electric current>, <chemical reactions> at that interface exchange <electrons> with <chemical species>.

= Anode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <anode> is the <electrode> where <oxidation> occurs. It is positive in the specified driven <electrolytic cell> and negative in a <galvanic cell> delivering electrical <work>.

= Cathode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <cathode> is the <electrode> where <reduction> occurs. It is negative in the specified driven <electrolytic cell> and positive in a <galvanic cell> delivering electrical <work>.

= Galvanic cell
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <galvanic cell> supplies electrical <work> from a spontaneous <redox> <chemical reaction>; <oxidation> occurs at its negative <anode> and <reduction> at its positive <cathode>.

= Electrolysis
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

<Electrolysis> uses an external electrical source to drive a <chemical reaction> in an <electrolytic cell>.

= Electrolytic cell
{parent=Electrolysis}
{wiki}

An <electrolytic cell> consumes electrical <work> to drive a <redox> <chemical reaction>. Its positive <anode> supports <oxidation>, and its negative <cathode> supports <reduction>.

= Electrolyte
{parent=Electrochemistry}

An <electrolyte> is a conducting medium in which mobile <ions> carry <electric current>, according to \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/09061][IUPAC Gold Book: electrolyte]{ref}. In introductory <chemistry>, a substance that yields <ions> when dissolved in <water> is also called an <electrolyte>. Both usages point to the same mechanism: an <electric field> gives mobile <cations> and <anions> directed <drift velocities>.

Mobility matters as much as the presence of <electric charge>. In a simple ideal model of solid <sodium chloride>, <ions> are confined to their sites and do not carry a sustained transport <electric current>. Molten <sodium chloride> has mobile <ions>; dissolved <sodium chloride> gives mobile $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <ions> in <water>. Real solids can have some ionic transport through imperfections; the immobile-solid picture is an introductory limit, rather than a claim that all solids are perfect insulators.

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from the dissolved substance, whereas a <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction under the specified conditions. Dissolved <sodium chloride> is a <strong electrolyte>. <Sucrose> is a <nonelectrolyte>: it dissolves as neutral <molecules>, so dissolving something does not automatically create mobile <ions>. <Water>'s own small population of <hydronium> and <hydroxide> remains possible. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-2-electrolytes][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §11.2: strong, weak, and nonelectrolytes]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §11.4, Table 11.3: sucrose and sodium chloride]{ref}.

<Acetic acid> is a <weak electrolyte> and a <weak acid>. In <water>, some neutral <acetic acid> <molecules> transfer a <proton> to <water>, producing <hydronium> and the negatively charged partner:
$$
\mathrm{CH_3COOH}+\mathrm{H_2O}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}+\mathrm{H_3O^+}.
$$
Both sides have net <electric charge> zero. At <chemical equilibrium>, neutral <acetic acid> coexists with these <ions>; the fraction converted depends on the conditions. <Electrolyte> strength describes this extent of ion production, while <concentration> describes how much substance is present per specified quantity of <chemical solution>. A dilute <strong electrolyte> can contain fewer <ions> per volume than a concentrated <weak electrolyte>. Neither the words strong and weak nor a conductivity comparison alone specifies <concentration>.

Now specify an <electrolytic cell> with a positive <anode> on the left, a negative <cathode> on the right, and an approximately uniform <electric field> pointing right in the bulk <electrolyte>. With a steady external source and neglecting <diffusion> and <convection>, positive <cations> drift right toward the negative <cathode>; negative <anions> drift left toward the positive <anode>. Yet both populations contribute a rightward <conventional current>! For <number densities> $n_+,n_-$ and <drift velocities> $\mathbf v_+,\mathbf v_-$, the transport <current density> is
$$
\mathbf J=n_+(+e)\mathbf v_++n_-(-e)\mathbf v_-.
$$
The product of <electric charge> and <velocity> determines <conventional current>: for the <anion>, both the charge and its rightward velocity component are negative, making its rightward contribution positive. This is the same sign rule used for <electron> <conventional current>. It follows directly from the existing <charge carrier> formula.

\Image[electrolyte-ion-drift-current.png]
{title=Opposite <ion> <drift velocities> contribute to the same <conventional current> in a specified <electrolytic cell>}
{description=The positive <anode> is left and the negative <cathode> right. The upper arrows show <ion> motion; the lower arrows show the signs of $nqv_x$ for <current density>. A uniform bulk <electric field> is assumed; <diffusion>, <convection>, and <electrode> boundary layers are omitted. Arrow lengths are schematic.}

For an independent numerical example, stipulate $n_+=n_-=10^{25}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $v_{+,x}=2\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and $v_{-,x}=-3\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. With the exact <elementary charge> $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$, the contributions are
$$
J_{+,x}=320.4353268\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_{-,x}=480.6529902\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_x=801.088317\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}}.
$$
Equal <number densities> of singly charged <cations> and <anions> give zero bulk <charge density>, while their motion gives nonzero <current density>. This is why a neutral <electrolyte> can conduct. These numbers are chosen toy inputs, not measurements of <sodium chloride>; maintaining sustained cell <electric current> also requires suitable <electrode> reactions. Blocking <electrodes> can instead lead to charge accumulation and decay of the <electric current>.

An <electrolyte> connects the microscopic motion of <ions> to observable <electrical conductivity>. A counterion-only model keeps just the mobile <counterions> needed to oppose a fixed <electric charge>; <Poisson-Boltzmann equations> use ideal <Boltzmann distributions> to model their equilibrium distributions, rather than the drift example above.

= Strong electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from its dissolved substance in the specified <solvent> and conditions. Strength does not specify <concentration>.

= Weak electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction of <ions> from its dissolved substance under the specified conditions, as illustrated by <acetic acid> in <water>.

= Nonelectrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}

A <nonelectrolyte> supplies neutral <molecules> rather than <ions> on dissolving in the specified <solvent>, as illustrated by <sucrose> in <water>.

= Chemical reaction
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical reaction> interconverts <chemical species>, as defined by the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01033][IUPAC Gold Book, chemical reaction]{ref}. The consumed species are <reactants>; the formed species are <reaction products>. In ordinary <chemical reactions>, <chemical atoms> are rearranged while each <chemical element>'s total atom count is conserved. For a complete <chemical equation>, net <electric charge> is conserved too.

For example, the balanced <chemical equation>
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}
$$
describes conversion of <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> into <water>. Both sides contain four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Three <reactant> <molecules> become two <reaction product> <molecules>: atom counts are conserved, but molecule counts need not be. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, balancing chemical equations]{ref}.

The coefficients describe net consumption and formation, without asserting that three <molecules> collide in one step. A balanced <chemical equation> supplies quantitative constraints; it does not determine how quickly the <chemical reaction> occurs, or whether <chemical equilibrium> prevents complete conversion. Those questions need further information. Keeping these roles distinct makes <stoichiometry> useful even when the microscopic details are complicated.

= Redox
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Oxidation-reduction reaction
{synonym}

A <redox> <chemical reaction> couples <oxidation> and <reduction>. In the <electrode> examples here, <oxidation> releases <electrons> and <reduction> accepts them. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-2-classifying-chemical-reactions][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.2: oxidation-reduction reactions]{ref}.

= Oxidation
{parent=Redox}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <oxidation> is loss of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <anode>.

= Reduction
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Redox}

= Reduction
{synonym}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <reduction> is gain of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <cathode>.

= Hydrolysis
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Hydrolysis> is a <chemical reaction> involving <water> that breaks a <chemical bond>. The <free energy> released by suitably coupled <ATP> <hydrolysis> can drive <active transport>; the available <free energy> depends on the actual composition and conditions.

= Energy coupling
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{title2=$\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta G_1+\Delta G_2$}

<Energy coupling> links an <endergonic reaction> to an <exergonic reaction> through a <reaction mechanism>, so that the combined transformation has a favourable change in <Gibbs free energy>. <ATP> <hydrolysis> is a common driving <chemical reaction>. A shared <reaction intermediate>, produced in one step and consumed in another, can physically connect the transformations; merely placing two independent <chemical reactions> in the same <chemical solution> does not establish <energy coupling>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: coupled reactions and activated intermediates]{ref}.

\Image[atp-energy-coupling.png]
{title=<Energy coupling>: $+20-30.5=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under the same stipulated conditions}
{description=Original diagram of cumulative changes in <Gibbs free energy> as two contributions are added. The levels are bookkeeping steps, not a <reaction mechanism>, actual <reaction intermediates>, elapsed time, or <activation energy> barriers. A <reaction mechanism> must enforce the joint transformation for the net favourable value to drive the desired conversion.}

For a computed example, stipulate an <endergonic reaction> $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with $\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under precisely the same biochemical <standard state> conditions as the quoted <ATP> <hydrolysis> value. Assume a <reaction mechanism> couples one <ATP> <hydrolysis> to each A-to-B conversion. The formal sum is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A&\longrightarrow\mathrm B,
&\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}&=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_2^{\circ\prime}&=-30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm A+\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm B+\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}^{\circ\prime}&=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The sum $+20+(-30.5)=-10.5$ gives an <exergonic reaction>. The $+20$ is a chosen teaching value, not a measured property of an identified substance. Outside these reference conditions, add the actual $\Delta G_1$ and $\Delta G_2$, each evaluated using consistent <thermodynamic activities>; mixing a standard value for one <chemical reaction> with an unrelated cellular value for the other gives no justified prediction.

Why do the changes in <Gibbs free energy> add? <Gibbs free energy> is a <state function>. Adding consistent reaction changes cancels any shared <reaction intermediate> and leaves the difference between the combined <reactants> and combined <reaction products>. This explains the thermodynamic accounting; it does not construct the <reaction mechanism>. In many <ATP>-dependent routes, <phosphorylation> transfers a <phosphate> group to a <substrate> or an <enzyme>, and subsequent transformation consumes that modified material. The shared material is what connects the steps. Other <reaction mechanisms> use <ATP> differently. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: ATP-driven biosynthesis]{ref}.

The useful achievement is that a common regenerated <nucleotide> can connect many different processes in <metabolism>. The net negative change in <Gibbs free energy> permits the coupled conversion, but supplies no <reaction rate>: <enzymes> can lower <activation energy> barriers without changing the endpoint difference. <Energy coupling> changes which overall <chemical reaction> is being considered; <catalysis> accelerates an available route. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: free-energy changes and reaction barriers]{ref}.

A historical landmark is Fritz Lipmann's \a[https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470122464.ch4][1941 essay, Metabolic Generation and Utilization of Phosphate Bond Energy]{ref}. Its sections on the <phosphate> cycle, generation and utilization of phosphate-bond <energy>, and group transfer organized these processes around a reusable chemical carrier. The historical terminology should be read with the modern distinction between net <hydrolysis> and the cost of breaking a <chemical bond>.

= Exergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G<0$}

An <exergonic reaction> has negative molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, its forward direction is thermodynamically favourable; its <reaction rate> remains a separate question. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: exergonic reactions]{ref}.

= Endergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G>0$}

An <endergonic reaction> has positive molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. Sustained net forward conversion needs a driving process, such as <energy coupling>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: endergonic reactions]{ref}.

= Phosphorylation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Phosphorylation> attaches a <phosphate> group to a <molecule>. Transfer from <ATP> to a <substrate> or <enzyme> can participate in <energy coupling>; the name alone does not specify the complete <reaction mechanism>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate][OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: phosphorylation]{ref}.

= Chemical kinetics
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Chemical kinetics> studies how quickly <chemical reactions> proceed and how their <reaction rates> depend on composition and conditions. It connects a measured change in <concentration> to a <rate law>, then uses a <rate equation> to predict future <concentrations>. This makes it useful for choosing how long to wait for conversion and for testing a proposed <reaction mechanism>. Changing <temperature>, <reactant> <concentrations>, or the presence of a <catalyst> can change <reaction rates>; see \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-2-factors-affecting-reaction-rates][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.2: factors affecting reaction rates]{ref}.

<Chemical equilibrium> specifies the eventual balance under given constraints; <chemical kinetics> determines how rapidly it is approached. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, a decrease in <Gibbs free energy> favours conversion but does not guarantee a fast <chemical reaction>: reaching a <transition state> can require crossing a substantial barrier. Thus a mixture can change imperceptibly over an observation period without being at <chemical equilibrium>. The distinction separates which direction is favoured from how long conversion takes. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-5-collision-theory][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.5: collision theory and reaction barriers]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-1-spontaneity][Chemistry 2e, §16.1: spontaneity and speed]{ref}.

= Activation energy
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$E_a$}

= Arrhenius activation energy
{c}
{synonym}

<Activation energy> quantifies how strongly a <rate constant> depends on <thermodynamic temperature>. For a positive <rate constant> $k(T)$, the empirical definition is
$$
E_a(T)=RT^2\frac{d}{dT}\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}},
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $k_{\mathrm{ref}}>0$ is a fixed reference with the same units as $k$. The ratio is a <dimensionless quantity>, keeping the <natural logarithm> meaningful without changing its <derivative>. Thus $E_a$ is a molar <energy>, commonly in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ or $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The <Arrhenius equation> makes $E_a$ constant over its assumed range. This rate-based definition, and the need for care when identifying it with a threshold on a <potential energy surface>, are stated in the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00102][IUPAC Gold Book: activation energy]{ref}.

In a simple barrier picture, reaching a <transition state> costs <energy> relative to the starting <reactants>. The height of that barrier is different from the difference between the <reactants> and <reaction products>. An energetically favourable endpoint can still be reached slowly. An <energy profile (chemistry)> makes the distinction visible, while an <Arrhenius plot> tests temperature sensitivity from <rate constants>. A measured $E_a$ need not equal a barrier read from an arbitrarily chosen diagram.

For a computed example, assume a <first-order reaction> has $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, constant $E_a=80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and a temperature-independent <pre-exponential factor>. The <Arrhenius equation> gives
$$
\frac{k(310\,\mathrm K)}{k(300\,\mathrm K)}
=\exp\left[\frac{80000}{8.314462618}\left(\frac1{300}-\frac1{310}\right)\right]
\approx2.81398.
$$
Hence $k(310\,\mathrm K)\approx0.281398\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its <half-life> falls from $\ln2/0.100\approx6.93147\,\mathrm s$ to $\ln2/0.281398\approx2.46322\,\mathrm s$. These are chosen model parameters, not measurements of an identified <chemical reaction>. The exponent uses <kelvin>, not unconverted Celsius temperatures, and $80000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ must match $R$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

= Arrhenius equation
{c}
{parent=Activation energy}
{wiki}
{title2=$k(T)=A\exp[-E_a/(RT)]$}

The <Arrhenius equation> models the <temperature> dependence of a <rate constant> by
$$
k(T)=A\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right),\qquad A>0,\quad T>0.
$$
Here $A$ is the <pre-exponential factor>, $E_a$ the <activation energy>, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, and $T$ the absolute <thermodynamic temperature>. In its original form, $A$ and $E_a$ are held constant over the range being modeled. It is an empirical relationship, not a universal rule for every <reaction mechanism>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00446][IUPAC Gold Book: Arrhenius equation]{ref}.

Eliminating $A$ between two positive <temperatures> gives the useful prediction
$$
\ln\frac{k(T_2)}{k(T_1)}
=\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}\right).
$$
For $E_a>0$, warming increases $k$. The logarithmic sensitivity is $d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})/dT=E_a/(RT^2)$, so there is no universal doubling per ten degrees. The same $10\,\mathrm K$ rise from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$ gives a factor $1.67749$ for $E_a=40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, versus $2.81398$ for $80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

\Image[arrhenius-temperature-sensitivity.png]
{title=Temperature sensitivity and straight-line <Arrhenius plots> for two illustrative <activation energies>}
{description=Both models are anchored at $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, so they have different <pre-exponential factors>. Left: the higher <activation energy> produces a larger relative change with <temperature>. Right: the <natural logarithm> of the dimensionless ratio $k(T)/k(300\,\mathrm K)$ is linear in inverse <temperature>. These exact model curves are not experimental data.}

The useful simplification is that a strong change in <reaction rate> becomes a straight-line relation after changing variables. Measurements at several <temperatures> can estimate the slope and test whether one pair of constants describes the chosen range. Curvature can indicate a failure of that approximation; fitting a line does not uniquely establish a microscopic <reaction mechanism>.

Svante Arrhenius's 1889 paper on acid-catalysed inversion of cane sugar compared the strong observed temperature dependence with the much smaller increase in collision frequency. He discussed van't Hoff's earlier temperature relations and proposed an activated-sugar hypothesis to interpret the dependence. That historical hypothesis should be distinguished from the modern <transition state> description. See the \a[https://webserver.lemoyne.edu/giunta/arrlaw.html][translated excerpt of Arrhenius, On the Reaction Velocity of the Inversion of Cane Sugar by Acids, Zeitschrift für Physikalische Chemie 4 (1889), pp. 226–248]{ref}.

= Pre-exponential factor
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$A$}

= Arrhenius prefactor
{c}
{synonym}

= Frequency factor
{synonym}

The <pre-exponential factor> multiplies the <exponential function> in the <Arrhenius equation>. It has the same units as the <rate constant>, so for a <first-order reaction> both have units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its physical interpretation depends on the <reaction mechanism>; an assumed constant $A$ should not be identified with a universal collision frequency. Comparing <activation energies> alone does not rank absolute <rate constants> when the <pre-exponential factors> differ. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04811][IUPAC Gold Book: pre-exponential factor]{ref}.

= Arrhenius plot
{c}
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})\ \text{against}\ 1/T$}

An <Arrhenius plot> places $\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$ against $1/T$, using a fixed reference <rate constant> with the same units as $k$. Under the constant-parameter <Arrhenius equation>,
$$
\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}
=\ln\frac{A}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}-\frac{E_a}{R}\frac1T.
$$
The slope is $-E_a/R$ and the intercept is $\ln(A/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$. Changing the reference units shifts the intercept but leaves the slope unchanged. If the horizontal coordinate is displayed in $10^{-3}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, its numerical slope must be rescaled before multiplying by $-R$. A temperature-dependent <pre-exponential factor> or <activation energy> can make the plot curved.

= Reaction mechanism
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <reaction mechanism> proposes the sequence of <elementary reactions> that produces an overall <chemical reaction>. Its predicted <rate law> must agree with observations; matching one <rate law> need not uniquely identify a <reaction mechanism>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-6-reaction-mechanisms][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.6: reaction mechanisms]{ref}.

= Reaction intermediate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

A <reaction intermediate> is formed in one step of a <reaction mechanism> and consumed in a later step, cancelling from the net <chemical equation>. A <reaction intermediate> can connect the transformations in <energy coupling>; it is distinct from a <transition state>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03096][IUPAC Gold Book: intermediate]{ref}.

= Reaction coordinate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}
{title2=$q$}

A <reaction coordinate> parametrizes changes in molecular configuration along a <chemical reaction> path. It can involve a <bond length>, a <bond angle>, or a combination of geometric changes. It is not elapsed time and is not the bulk <extent of reaction>: one describes configurations, while the other accounts for how much material has converted. The precise choice matters when interpreting an <energy profile (chemistry)>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05168][IUPAC Gold Book: reaction coordinate]{ref}.

= Potential energy surface
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}
{title2=$V(\mathbf R_1,\ldots,\mathbf R_N)$}

A <potential energy surface> assigns <potential energy> to the configurations of the reacting <chemical atoms>, for a specified electronic description. A <reaction coordinate> selects a path through this many-dimensional configuration space; a <potential-energy profile> then records the <potential energy> along that path. Different paths can share endpoints but cross different barriers. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04780][IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy reaction surface]{ref}.

= Energy profile
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}

= Reaction energy profile
{synonym}

An <energy profile (chemistry)> plots an explicitly specified <energy> against a <reaction coordinate>. Identify whether the vertical axis is <potential energy>, <enthalpy>, or <Gibbs free energy>; these descriptions are related but not interchangeable. A <potential-energy profile> describes a path through configurations, while a free-energy description also accounts for the relevant thermodynamic conditions.

\Image[reaction-barriers-and-endpoints.png]
{title=Two schematic <potential-energy profiles> share endpoints but have different barriers}
{description=The reactant level is $0$, the product level is $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and the two maxima are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. These chosen curves illustrate endpoint differences and barrier heights; they are not measured surfaces, trajectories in time, or a complete catalytic cycle.}

For the two chosen profiles, the <reaction energy barriers> from the <reactants> are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. From the <reaction products>, the same maxima are $100$ and $70\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ higher. Both paths have endpoint difference $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The barrier is measured from the relevant starting endpoint, not from an arbitrary zero of <potential energy>.

The appeal of the diagram is that it separates two useful questions: which endpoint is lower, and which path is easier to traverse? <Catalysis> changes the route through <transition states> while retaining the endpoint thermodynamics of the same overall <chemical reaction>. A real catalytic route may have several steps and <reaction intermediates>; the two simple curves do not specify such a <reaction mechanism>. The plotted potential-energy difference alone is not an <equilibrium constant> or a calculated <rate constant>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis][Chemistry 2e, 12.7: alternative reaction routes and catalysis]{ref}.

= Potential-energy profile
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}

A <potential-energy profile> plots the <potential energy> of the reacting system along a specified path from <reactants> through a <transition state> to <reaction products>. It is a particular kind of <energy profile (chemistry)>. For an <elementary reaction>, a suitable <reaction coordinate> parametrizes the path; a multi-step <reaction mechanism> needs the coordinates of its successive steps. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04779][IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy profile]{ref}.

= Reaction energy barrier
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}
{title2=$\Delta E^\ddagger=E_{\mathrm{peak}}-E_{\mathrm{start}}$}

A <reaction energy barrier> is the height of a specified peak relative to the starting state in an <energy profile (chemistry)>. State which <energy> is used and which direction is being considered. A schematic barrier is not automatically the empirical <activation energy> obtained from the temperature dependence of a <rate constant>.

= Elementary reaction
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

An <elementary reaction> is a single step in a <reaction mechanism>. The <law of mass action> models its directional <reaction rate> using <reactant> <concentrations> raised to the step's stoichiometric multiplicities. Applying that rule requires evidence that the step is elementary; a net <chemical equation> alone supplies no such evidence.

= Catalysis
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

<Catalysis> accelerates a <chemical reaction> by providing an alternative <reaction mechanism> with a more accessible barrier through its <transition states>. A <catalyst> participates in the route and is regenerated. At fixed <temperature>, <catalysis> changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is reached without changing the <equilibrium constant>; it does not make an unfavourable overall change in <Gibbs free energy> favourable. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.7: catalysis]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle][Chemistry 2e, §13.3: effect of a catalyst on equilibrium]{ref}.

= Catalyst
{parent=Catalysis}

A <catalyst> makes a <chemical reaction> proceed faster by participating in an alternative <reaction mechanism> and being regenerated. This makes repeated conversion possible: the same <catalyst> can help many successive <reactants> become <reaction products>. An <enzyme> is a biological <catalyst>. The overall standard change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged; see the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00876][IUPAC Gold Book: catalyst]{ref}.

Regeneration does not mean that the <catalyst> sits unchanged throughout every step. For example, a schematic <reaction mechanism> can temporarily bind A to a <catalyst> C in a <reaction intermediate> AC:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A+\mathrm C&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{AC},\\
\mathrm{AC}&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B+\mathrm C,\\
\text{net:}\qquad\mathrm A&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B.
\end{aligned}
$$
Adding the steps cancels both the <reaction intermediate> AC and the <catalyst> C from the net <chemical equation>. AC is produced and later consumed; C is used and later recovered. These letters describe possible roles, not a measured <reaction mechanism>. The alternative route is useful only if its <chemical kinetics> permits faster conversion. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.7: catalysts and alternative mechanisms]{ref}.

For a worked barrier example, consider an idealized reversible A-to-B conversion at fixed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. Stipulate standard molar <Gibbs free energy> levels $G_{\mathrm A}^\circ=0$ and $G_{\mathrm B}^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, with effective <transition state> levels $80$ without a <catalyst> and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ with a <catalyst>. Use the same <standard states> and the same zero for both routes; the catalytic route includes the regenerated C at both endpoints. Unlike the separate <potential-energy profile> example, this illustration uses <Gibbs free energy> at one specified <temperature>.

\Image[catalyst-alternative-reaction-path.png]
{title=A <catalyst> lowers the stipulated <Gibbs energy of activation> in both directions while retaining the same endpoints}
{description=Original smooth schematic <Gibbs free energy> profiles at $300\,\mathrm K$, with levels in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The horizontal <reaction coordinate> describes progress along each schematic route, not time. These are chosen effective barriers, not measured <potential energy surfaces>, actual molecular paths, or the two-step <reaction mechanism> above. A real catalytic route can have several <transition states> and <reaction intermediates>.}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is measured from the starting endpoint in the chosen direction. Thus the four barriers are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-0=80,&
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-0=50,\\
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-(-20)=100,&
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-(-20)=70,
\end{aligned}
\qquad\text{all in }\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Both directional barriers fall by $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The endpoint difference stays $\Delta_rG^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

To turn those barriers into <rate constants>, use the Eyring form of <transition state theory> for this effective <first-order reaction> pair, with a common prefactor and unit probability of successful passage:
$$
k=\nu\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}}{RT}\right),
\qquad \nu=\frac{k_{\mathrm B}T}{h}.
$$
Here $k_{\mathrm B}$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $h$ is the <Planck constant>, and $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. The common prefactor cancels from the comparisons. These <Gibbs energies of activation> are not the empirical <activation energy> $E_a$ of the <Arrhenius equation>. With the stipulated value $R=8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, the speedup is
$$
\frac{k_{f,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\frac{k_{r,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\exp\left(\frac{30000}{8.314462618\times300}\right)
\approx1.67248\times10^5.
$$
The exponent is a <dimensionless quantity>: convert $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ to $30000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ before dividing by $RT$. The <exponential function> explains why a modest barrier reduction can have a dramatic kinetic effect. This number follows from the chosen free-energy barriers and common prefactor; it is not a universal catalytic multiplier or a prediction across <temperatures>.

\Image[catalyst-forward-reverse-rate-ratios.png]
{title=Both directional <rate constants> increase by about $1.67\times10^5$, while their forward-to-reverse ratio stays about $3036$}
{description=Original bar plots at $300\,\mathrm K$. Left: base-ten <logarithms> of the <dimensionless> normalized <rate constants> $k/\nu$ for the four stipulated barriers. A less negative value means faster conversion. Right: the <logarithm> of $k_f/k_r$ is identical for the two routes. Absolute units factor out through the common $\nu=k_{\mathrm B}T/h$; no temperature dependence outside this one model point is asserted.}

Assume ideal <thermodynamic activities> with a common concentration <standard state>, and directional <rate laws> $J_f=k_f[\mathrm A]$ and $J_r=k_r[\mathrm B]$. At <chemical equilibrium>, equal <reaction rates> give $K=[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]=k_f/k_r$ for this model. The barriers give
$$
K_{\mathrm{uncat}}=\frac{e^{-80000/(RT)}}{e^{-100000/(RT)}}
=e^{20000/(RT)}
=\frac{e^{-50000/(RT)}}{e^{-70000/(RT)}}=K_{\mathrm{cat}}
\approx3035.58.
$$
The <catalyst> speeds up both directions, so it changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is approached without changing this <equilibrium constant>. For a general <chemical reaction>, the thermodynamic relation is $K=\exp[-\Delta_rG^\circ/(RT)]$ with consistent <standard states>; an arbitrary multistep <reaction mechanism> need not permit $K=k_f/k_r$ for two measured <rate constants>. At the same <temperature>, <pressure>, and <thermodynamic activities>, a <catalyst> also leaves the actual overall reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> unchanged. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle][Chemistry 2e, §13.3: a catalyst does not shift equilibrium]{ref}. To drive an unfavourable overall conversion, a process such as <energy coupling> must change the combined transformation.

= Transition state theory
{parent=Catalyst}
{wiki}
{title2=$k=(k_{\mathrm B}T/h)\exp[-\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}/(RT)]$}

<Transition state theory> relates an <elementary reaction>'s <rate constant> to a <Gibbs energy of activation>. The Eyring expression used in the <catalyst> example assumes every passage counted at the <transition state> succeeds. More generally, a factor accounting for unsuccessful passage multiplies it. Comparing barriers alone gives the quoted speedup only when the remaining prefactors agree. Free-energy profiles can depend on <temperature>, so the example's values at $300\,\mathrm K$ do not determine a temperature curve. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06470][IUPAC Gold Book: transition state theory]{ref}.

= Gibbs energy of activation
{c}
{parent=Transition state theory}
{title2=$\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}$}

= Gibbs energies of activation
{c}
{synonym}

= Activation free energy
{synonym}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is the standard molar <Gibbs free energy> change associated with going from the starting <reactants> to the <transition state> in <transition state theory>. It specifies the directional barrier, rather than the overall endpoint change in <Gibbs free energy>. Use consistent <standard states> when comparing <Gibbs energies of activation> and <rate constants>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/G02631][IUPAC Gold Book: Gibbs energy of activation]{ref}.

= Transition state
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <transition state> describes configurations at the barrier between <reactants> and <reaction products> in an <elementary reaction>. It is a crossing region, rather than a stable substance that accumulates in a bottle. The IUPAC definition characterizes these configurations by equal probabilities of proceeding toward <reactants> or <reaction products>: \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06468][IUPAC Gold Book: transition state]{ref}. Lowering the barrier relative to the starting configuration can increase a <reaction rate>, even when the overall change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged.

= Reaction rate
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$v$}
{wiki}

For one <chemical reaction> in a <closed system> of fixed <volume> $V$, with uniform <concentrations> and no competing reactions or appreciable accumulation of unlisted species, choose the <chemical equation>
$$
a\mathrm A+b\mathrm B\longrightarrow c\mathrm C,\qquad a,b,c>0.
$$
Brackets denote <amount of substance> per <volume>, for example $[\mathrm A]=n_{\mathrm A}/V$. The <stoichiometry> gives one instantaneous <reaction rate>
$$
v=-\frac1a\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}
=-\frac1b\frac{d[\mathrm B]}{dt}
=\frac1c\frac{d[\mathrm C]}{dt}
=\frac1V\frac{d\xi}{dt},
$$
where $\xi$ is the existing <extent of reaction>. The minus signs make forward consumption positive; dividing by the coefficients makes measurements of different <chemical species> agree. Units can be $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05156][IUPAC Gold Book: rate of reaction]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-1-chemical-reaction-rates][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.1: chemical reaction rates]{ref}.

For the illustrative <chemical equation> $2\mathrm A+\mathrm B\longrightarrow3\mathrm C$, a measured $d[\mathrm C]/dt=0.060\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ implies $v=0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-0.040\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, and $d[\mathrm B]/dt=-0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These numbers express <stoichiometry>, without supplying a <rate law>.

Each <derivative> is the slope of a <concentration> curve at one instant. Over a finite interval, $-\Delta[\mathrm A]/(a\Delta t)$ instead gives an average <reaction rate>. For A converting reversibly to B, the net <reaction rate> is $v=d[\mathrm B]/dt=J_f-J_r$; both directional <reaction rates> can remain positive when $v=0$ at <chemical equilibrium>.

= Rate law
{parent=Reaction rate}

A <rate law> specifies how a <reaction rate> depends on <concentrations> under stated conditions. A common form is
$$
v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta,
$$
where $k$ is a <rate constant> and the exponents are partial <orders of reaction>. Determine them experimentally, or derive them from a justified <reaction mechanism>. Do not infer $\alpha=a$ and $\beta=b$ from the net <chemical equation>: its coefficients describe consumption ratios, whereas the exponents describe sensitivity to <concentration>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05141][IUPAC Gold Book: rate law]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.3: rate laws]{ref}.

A <rate law> becomes a prediction of time evolution when combined with <stoichiometry> in a <rate equation>, for example $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-av$. Agreement with a measured <rate law> is useful evidence for a <reaction mechanism>, but several routes can give the same dependence.

= Order of reaction
{parent=Rate law}
{title2=$n$}

= Orders of reaction
{synonym}

= Reaction order
{synonym}

In a power-form <rate law> $v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta$, the partial <orders of reaction> are $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and the overall <order of reaction> is $n=\alpha+\beta$. For example, doubling $[\mathrm A]$ at fixed $[\mathrm B]$ multiplies $v$ by $2^\alpha$: factors of one, two, and four correspond to partial <orders of reaction> zero, one, and two. These exponents can also be fractional or negative. A single constant <order of reaction> need not describe a <rate law> that is not a power form. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322][IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction]{ref}.

For a simple irreversible conversion $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with <rate equation> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k[\mathrm A]^n$, initial <concentration> $A_0>0$, and fixed $k>0$, compare <zero-order reactions>, <first-order reactions>, and <second-order reactions> using the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
X=\frac{[\mathrm A]}{A_0},\qquad \tau=kA_0^{n-1}t,\qquad \frac{dX}{d\tau}=-X^n.
$$
Their integrated curves, starting at $X(0)=1$, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
n=0:&\quad X=1-\tau\quad(0\leq\tau<1),\\
n=1:&\quad X=e^{-\tau},\\
n=2:&\quad X=\frac1{1+\tau}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the <zero-order reaction>, the plotted extension $X=\max(1-\tau,0)$ stops conversion at depletion. It does not continue to obey $dX/d\tau=-1$ once $X=0$. A negative <concentration> would be unphysical. The <first-order reaction> and <second-order reaction> approach zero without reaching it at finite time in these models.

\Image[reaction-order-concentration-curves.png]
{title=<Dimensionless> <concentration> decays for three <orders of reaction>, with <zero-order reaction> depletion and the corresponding linearizing plots}

The common $\tau$ axis compares curve shapes after each model's own time rescaling, rather than equal laboratory times for an identical numerical $k$. In the lower panels, $X$, $\ln X$, and $1/X$ respectively give straight lines with slopes $-1$, $-1$, and $+1$ for the matching <order of reaction>. The <natural logarithm> uses a <dimensionless quantity>. These transformed plots help test a proposed <rate law>; the <zero-order reaction> line applies only before depletion. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-4-integrated-rate-laws][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.4: integrated rate laws]{ref}.

= First-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <first-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> proportional to one <reactant> <concentration> in the simple single-species case. For an irreversible $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ in a <closed system> at fixed <volume>, take $[\mathrm A](0)=A_0$, $[\mathrm B](0)=0$, and constant <rate constant> $k>0$. Its <rate equation> and solution are
$$
\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=-k[\mathrm A],\qquad
[\mathrm A](t)=A_0e^{-kt},\qquad
[\mathrm B](t)=A_0(1-e^{-kt}),\qquad
v(t)=kA_0e^{-kt}.
$$
A short check is enough: differentiating $A_0e^{-kt}$ gives $-kA_0e^{-kt}$, and the two <concentrations> sum to $A_0$, as required by the one-to-one <stoichiometry>. Thus the same <exponential decay> describes both remaining <reactant> and decreasing <reaction rate>. Fixed $k$ means a fixed fractional loss per unit time, not a fixed absolute consumption rate.

Choose illustrative values $A_0=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, with $t$ measured in seconds. Then $[\mathrm A]=e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]=(1-e^{-0.2t})\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v=0.2e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. The <half-life> is
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{\ln2}{k}=\frac{\ln2}{0.2}\,\mathrm s\approx3.4657\,\mathrm s.
$$
At each successive <half-life>, half the remaining <reactant> is consumed; $t_{1/2}$ is independent of $A_0$ for this <first-order reaction>. At $t=10\,\mathrm s$, the calculated values are $[\mathrm A]\approx0.135335\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]\approx0.864665\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v\approx0.027067\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These are model predictions for chosen parameters, rather than experimental measurements.

\Image[chemical-first-order-reaction.png]
{title=Illustrative <first-order reaction>: <reactant> loss, <reaction product> formation, and declining <reaction rate> for $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$}

The left panel tracks the two <concentrations>; the right tracks their instantaneous conversion <reaction rate>. Their different vertical units matter: $v=k[\mathrm A]$, so the numerical curves need not have the same height. This gives a practical way to translate a measured <concentration> into a forecast of both conversion and waiting time.

= Zero-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <zero-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> independent of the relevant <reactant> <concentration> over the range where its <rate law> applies. For $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$, $[\mathrm A]=A_0-kt$ is valid only before depletion at $t=A_0/k$. The constant-consumption model must stop or change there.

= Second-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <second-order reaction> has overall <order of reaction> two. The simple <rate equation> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k[\mathrm A]^2$ gives $1/[\mathrm A]=1/A_0+kt$. An overall <second-order reaction> can instead involve two different <reactants>, for example $v=k[\mathrm A][\mathrm B]$; its <concentration> curves need not have the single-species form above.

= Rate constant
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$k$}

A <rate constant> is the coefficient in a specified <rate law>, held fixed under specified conditions. It can describe an empirical overall <chemical reaction>, as well as an <elementary reaction>. Fixed <temperature> matters: changing <temperature> can change $k$, even for the same <chemical reaction>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322][IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction and rate constants]{ref} and \a[https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/R/R05137.html][IUPAC Gold Book: rate coefficient]{ref}.

For $v=k[\mathrm A]^n$, dimensional consistency requires
$$
\operatorname{units}(k)=(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}})^{1-n}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.
$$
A <zero-order reaction> therefore uses $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$; a <first-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; a <second-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Comparing bare numerical values of $k$ across different <orders of reaction> is meaningless without their units and <concentrations>. Even for the same <order of reaction>, state whether $k$ multiplies the normalized <reaction rate> $v$ or a particular <reactant> disappearance rate: a stoichiometric coefficient can change that convention.

= Protonation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Chemical equation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

A <chemical equation> represents a <chemical reaction> using <chemical formulas>, with <reactants> on the left and <reaction products> on the right. A balanced <chemical equation> has the same number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> on both sides. For <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> forming <water>,
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}.
$$
The coefficients $2,1,2$ give the relative numbers of <molecules>; the coefficient $1$ is normally omitted. Count <hydrogen> as $2\times2=4$ on the left and $2\times2=4$ on the right, and <oxygen> as $1\times2=2$ on the left and $2\times1=2$ on the right. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.1: writing and balancing chemical equations]{ref}.

\Image[chemical-equation-atom-balance.png]
{title=Two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and one <molecular oxygen> <molecule> account for two <water> <molecules>}
{description=Exactly four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> occur on each side. The circles and connecting lines are an invented schematic: they indicate grouping and <chemical bonds>, without representing actual <bond lengths>, <bond angles> or a <reaction mechanism>.}

To balance the initial expression $\mathrm H_2+\mathrm O_2\longrightarrow\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, first put $2$ before <water> to match the two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. This creates four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> on the right, so put $2$ before <molecular hydrogen>. Changing a coefficient multiplies the entire <chemical formula>. Changing a subscript changes composition and usually the <chemical substance>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ is two <water> <molecules>, whereas $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$ is one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Never balance a <chemical equation> by changing its subscripts.

The bookkeeping follows from conservation of the <atomic nuclei> in ordinary, non-nuclear <chemical reactions>; <chemical bonds> and the distribution of <electrons> can change. For a <chemical equation> involving <ions>, total <electric charge> must also match. For example, the existing <self-ionization of water> equation has zero total <electric charge> on each side, since the $+1$ of <hydronium> cancels the $-1$ of <hydroxide>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-6-ionic-and-molecular-compounds][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.6: nuclei and ions]{ref}.

The same $2:1:2$ ratio relates changes in <amount of substance>: two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> consumed per <mole> of <molecular oxygen> correspond to two <moles> of <water> formed. A <mole> is a defined unit, not one <molecule>; the little whole-number picture is an abstract counting model, not a claim that a macroscopic sample contains just five <molecules>. See <molecular count and amount of substance> for the exact conversion and \a[https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole][BIPM, SI base unit: mole]{ref} for the definition.

This is why a balanced <chemical equation> is useful: <stoichiometry> turns the same atom accounting into relations between measurable quantities. It does not determine <reaction rate>, the final conversion set by <chemical equilibrium>, or the actual yield of recovered <reaction products>. The <limiting reagent> gives an upper bound under the specified <chemical reaction>.

= Reactant
{parent=Chemical reaction}

A <chemical species> consumed in a <chemical reaction>.

= Reagent
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Product
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction product
{synonym}

= Substrate
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction substrate
{synonym}

A chemical species on which a reagent or catalyst acts in a <chemical reaction>. An <enzyme> acts on a biochemical <substrate>, a particular instance of this broader reaction role.

= Thermal dissociation
{parent=Chemical reaction}

Thermal dissociation is temperature-driven breaking of a molecule into simpler fragments. The <chemical equilibrium> abundance depends on pressure as well as temperature: reactions increasing particle number are generally favored at low pressure. In an <exoplanet atmosphere>, reduced <water> on a very hot dayside does not by itself establish a low planetary oxygen abundance.

= Stoichiometry
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nu_i$}

<Stoichiometry> relates the quantities consumed and formed in a <chemical reaction>. Its coefficients are ratios of entity counts, or equivalently of <amount of substance>, rather than ratios of <masses>. For the <water> <chemical equation>, consuming two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> per <molecular oxygen> <molecule> gives the same $2:1$ ratio as consuming two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> per <mole> of <molecular oxygen>. Multiplying every entity count by the same factor preserves the ratio. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-3-reaction-stoichiometry][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, reaction stoichiometry]{ref}.

Coefficients and subscripts do different jobs. The coefficient in $2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$ counts two <water> <molecules>; the subscript says that each contains two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. Changing coefficients scales quantities. Changing a subscript changes the specified <chemical substance>.

\Image[stoichiometry-particle-counts.png]
{title=The same stoichiometric ratio at molecular and molar scales}
{description=The upper schematic counts individual <molecules>; the lower line multiplies each count by the <Avogadro constant> times one <mole>. Circle sizes and positions are illustrative.}

This distinction catches a common counting error: one <mole> of <water> <molecules> contains two <moles> of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and one <mole> of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Always specify what is counted.

<Stoichiometry> records reactant and product multiplicities in a <chemical reaction> or a <chemical reaction network>. The change caused by one reaction is its <stoichiometric vector>; multiplying by the reaction rate gives its contribution to the concentration equations. For two identical reactants the rate-constant convention absorbs any combinatorial factor.

= Molecular count and amount of substance
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$N=nN_{\mathrm A}$}

The molecular count $N$ is a dimensionless integer; the <amount of substance> $n$ has unit <mole>, symbol $\mathrm{mol}$. They satisfy
$$
N=nN_{\mathrm A},\qquad N_{\mathrm A}=6.02214076\times10^{23}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.
$$
The <Avogadro constant> is exact by the modern definition of the <mole>: see \a[https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole][BIPM, SI base unit: mole]{ref}. Thus two <water> <molecules> correspond to $2/N_{\mathrm A}$ <moles>, whereas two <moles> of <water> contain $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <molecules>. A <mole> is a unit for counting specified entities, which may also be <chemical atoms>, <ions> or <electrons>.

= Extent of reaction
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$\xi$}
{wiki}

For a closed system with one specified <chemical reaction>, the <extent of reaction> $\xi$ records progress from $\xi=0$ through $n_i=n_{i,0}+\nu_i\xi$. Here $n_i$ is the <amount of substance> of species $i$, and the signed <stoichiometric vector> has negative entries for <reactants> and positive entries for <reaction products>. For the <water> <chemical equation> in the order $\mathrm{H}_2,\mathrm{O}_2,\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$, it is $(-2,-1,2)$. The normalization of $\xi$ depends on the chosen coefficients. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02283][IUPAC Gold Book, extent of reaction]{ref}.

= Limiting reagent
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{wiki}

= Limiting reactant
{synonym}

The <limiting reagent> is the <reactant> exhausted first if the specified <chemical reaction> proceeds to the maximum conversion permitted by <stoichiometry>. With initial values of <amount of substance> $n_{i,0}$ for the <reactants> and positive consumption coefficients $a_i$, the largest forward <extent of reaction> is $\xi_{\max}=\min_i(n_{i,0}/a_i)$. Compare these quotients, rather than simply choosing the smallest initial quantity. This calculation predicts the maximum <reaction product> quantity and identifies which <reactant> supply must increase to raise it. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-4-reaction-yields][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, limiting reactants]{ref}.

Start with $3\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>, $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> and no <water>. Assume complete conversion by the displayed <chemical reaction>, without competing reactions. Then
$$
\xi_{\max}=\min\left(\frac{3\,\mathrm{mol}}2,\frac{1\,\mathrm{mol}}1\right)=1\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The <molecular oxygen> is the <limiting reagent>. Consumption of $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen> and $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> forms $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <water>, leaving $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>. Initially there are $1.806642228\times10^{24}$ <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ <molecular oxygen> <molecules>; after conversion there are $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <water> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ leftover <molecular hydrogen> <molecules>.

\Image[limiting-reagent-amounts.png]
{title=Oxygen runs out while one mole of molecular hydrogen remains}
{description=The horizontal axis is <extent of reaction>, rather than time. The endpoint assumes complete conversion by one <chemical reaction>; the curves express <stoichiometry>.}

As a check, the initial inventory has $6\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. The final <water> contains $4\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and leftover <molecular hydrogen> supplies the remaining $2\,\mathrm{mol}$. The atom totals balance even though the total <amount of substance> of <molecules> falls from four to three <moles>.

= Acid-base chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Acid-base chemistry> studies how <chemical acids> and <chemical bases> exchange <protons> or <electron pairs>. Here the focus is transfer of <protons> in <water>: a <chemical acid> donates a <proton>, and a <chemical base> accepts it. The resulting <chemical equilibrium> determines <pH>, while a <buffer solution> can resist changes in <pH>. This connects microscopic <chemical reactions> with a compact, measurable description of a <chemical solution>.

The important distinction is between how much <chemical acid> is present and how readily it transfers a <proton>. A <weak acid> and a <strong acid> at the same total <concentration> need not have the same <pH>. Conversely, two <buffer solutions> can have the same <pH> while containing very different total quantities of their <chemical acids> and <chemical bases>.

= Acid
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Acid}

= Chemical acid
{synonym}

A <chemical acid> can donate a <proton>, or accept an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>. The <pH> and <buffer solution> examples below use the former meaning. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00071][IUPAC Gold Book, acid]{ref}.

= Strong acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

In <water>, a <strong acid> transfers its available <proton> essentially completely to <water> under the dilute conditions considered here. For the <pH> example, each initial <chemical acid> unit supplies one <hydronium> <ion>. “Strong” describes this conversion, rather than a high <concentration>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-3-relative-strengths-of-acids-and-bases][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, relative strengths of acids and bases]{ref}.

= Weak acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

A <weak acid> transfers only part of its available <protons> to <water>, leaving both forms at <chemical equilibrium>. Its <acid dissociation constant> and the composition determine the <hydronium> <concentration>; substituting total <chemical acid> <concentration> into the <pH> formula is generally incorrect.

= Acid dissociation constant
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$K_a$}

For $\mathrm{HA}$ transferring a <proton> to <water>, the <acid dissociation constant> is the <equilibrium constant> written, when the <thermodynamic activity> of <water> is approximated by one, as $K_a=a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}/a_{\mathrm{HA}}$. Define $\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$. All <thermodynamic activities> and $K_a$ here are <dimensionless quantities>, with consistent <standard states>.

= pKa
{c}
{parent=Acid dissociation constant}
{title2=$\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$}

The <pKa> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of the <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constant>. A lower <pKa> means a larger <acid dissociation constant> and a stronger tendency to donate a <proton> under the same <standard state>, <solvent>, and <temperature> conventions. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> places equal <weak acid> and <conjugate base> <thermodynamic activities> at <pH> equal to <pKa>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation]{ref}.

= Base
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical base
{synonym}

A <chemical base> can accept a <proton>, using an available <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>; more generally it can donate an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond> with another species. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00601][IUPAC Gold Book, base]{ref}.

= Conjugate base
{parent=Base (chemistry)}

A <conjugate base> is what remains after a <chemical acid> donates one <proton>. In the schematic pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, $\mathrm{HA}$ is the <chemical acid> and $\mathrm A^-$ its <conjugate base>; accepting a <proton> reverses the conversion.

= pH
{c}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+}$}

The <pH> of a <chemical solution> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of its <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+},\qquad a_{\mathrm H^+}=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}.
$$
The <thermodynamic activity> is a <dimensionless quantity>, so the <logarithm> has a <dimensionless> argument. The definition uses <thermodynamic activity>, rather than total <chemical acid> <concentration>. Single-<ion> <thermodynamic activities> require a convention; practical <pH> measurements are tied to calibrated standards. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04524][IUPAC Gold Book, pH]{ref}.

In a dilute ideal <chemical solution> in <water>, a useful approximation is
$$
a_{\mathrm H^+}\approx\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{c^\circ},\qquad c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}}\right).
$$
Square brackets denote <concentration> in <moles> per litre. In <water>, $[\mathrm H^+]$ is shorthand for the <hydronium> <concentration>, often written $[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]$; it does not describe isolated bare <protons>. Departures from ideal behavior need <activity coefficients>. The <concentration> formula approximates the <thermodynamic activity> definition.

For an ideal <strong acid> supplying one <proton> per initial unit, stipulate a total <concentration> of $0.001\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and negligible <self-ionization of water>. Then $[\mathrm H^+]\approx10^{-3}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, so
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}(10^{-3})=3.
$$
Reducing that <concentration> tenfold by <dilution> gives $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and <pH> $4$, under the same assumptions. More generally, dividing the <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> by ten raises <pH> by one. This is why the scale compresses an enormous range: equal steps of <pH> represent multiplicative changes, rather than equal differences in <concentration>. These are model calculations, rather than measured samples.

\Image[ph-logarithmic-scale.png]
{title=One step of <pH> represents a tenfold change in <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>}
{description=The <logarithm> makes equal spacing on the <pH> axis correspond to factors of ten on the <thermodynamic activity> axis. The marked <strong acid> example uses the dilute ideal <concentration> approximation and negligible <self-ionization of water>. The plotted interval is illustrative; it does not bound possible <pH> values.}

Neutrality depends on <temperature>. In the dilute ideal <water> model, define the <dimensionless> ion product $K_w=([\mathrm H^+]/c^\circ)([\mathrm{OH}^-]/c^\circ)$ and $\mathrm pK_w=-\log_{10}K_w$. Equal <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <concentrations> imply
$$
\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{neutral}}=\frac{\mathrm pK_w}{2}.
$$
At $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, the textbook approximation $K_w\approx10^{-14}$ gives neutral <pH> $7$. Since $K_w$ varies with <temperature>, neutrality need not mean <pH> $7$. Nor is <pH> restricted to $0$–$14$: the activity definition permits negative <pH> when $a_{\mathrm H^+}>1$, and <pH> above $14$ when $a_{\mathrm H^+}<10^{-14}$. High <concentrations> can invalidate the dilute ideal approximation. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-2-ph-and-poh][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, pH and pOH]{ref}.

<S. P. L. Sørensen> introduced <pH> at the Carlsberg Laboratory in 1909, as recorded in the \a[https://carlsbergfondet.dk/en/about-the-foundation/our-history/timeline-of-the-foundation-s-history/][Carlsberg Foundation historical timeline]{ref}.

= Buffer solution
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

A <buffer solution> contains appreciable quantities of a <weak acid> and its <conjugate base>, and resists changes in <pH> caused by small quantities of added <chemical acid> or <chemical base>. The <conjugate base> accepts added <protons>; the <weak acid> supplies <protons> when a <chemical base> consumes them. Consuming most of either partner undermines the buffering action. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffers]{ref}.

For a separate toy calculation, stipulate <pKa> $5$ for a <weak acid> $\mathrm{HA}$; this is an input, not measured material data. Initially $1\,\mathrm L$ of <buffer solution> contains $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{HA}$ and $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of its <conjugate base> $\mathrm A^-$. In the ideal <concentration> approximation, the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> gives initial <pH> $5$. Add $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> with negligible volume change. The dominant <neutralization> has one-to-one <stoichiometry>:
$$
\mathrm{HA}+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm A^-+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O.
$$
Each <mole> of consumed <hydroxide> removes one <mole> of <weak acid> and forms one <mole> of <conjugate base> and <water>. Thus the inventories after <neutralization> are
$$
n_{\mathrm{HA}}\approx(0.10-0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.09\,\mathrm{mol},\qquad
n_{\mathrm A^-}\approx(0.10+0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.11\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The shared volume cancels from the <concentration> ratio. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> therefore predicts
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{0.11}{0.09}\right)=5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{11}{9}\right)=5.0871501757\ldots.
$$
This small increase illustrates how conversion between abundant partners absorbs added <chemical base>. The calculation neglects the tiny free <hydronium> and <hydroxide> inventories, changes during re-establishment of <chemical equilibrium>, <self-ionization of water>, and <activity coefficient> corrections. The displayed inventories approximate the final <chemical species> distribution; they are not an exact solution of all <chemical equilibrium> conditions.

\Image[buffer-neutralization-inventory.png]
{title=Added <hydroxide> transfers a finite inventory from <weak acid> to <conjugate base>}
{description=Toy <pKa> $5$, initial volume $1\,\mathrm L$, and negligible added volume. Bars show the dominant <neutralization> bookkeeping before and after adding $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide>; the <pH> values use the ideal <concentration> approximation. The acid-plus-base inventory stays $0.20\,\mathrm{mol}$. Neither the bars nor the <pH> values are measured material data or exact speciation.}

A <buffer solution> has a finite <buffer capacity>: its <weak acid> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical base>, and its <conjugate base> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical acid>. Here an addition approaching $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> uses up the available $\mathrm{HA}$ in the dominant <stoichiometry> calculation. Close to that limit, the neglected <chemical equilibrium> contributions matter; once $\mathrm{HA}$ is exhausted, inserting a zero denominator into the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> cannot predict the actual <pH>. Excess <hydroxide> and the remaining <chemical species> require a fresh <chemical equilibrium> calculation. This is why a <buffer solution> stabilizes conditions for <chemical reactions> over a finite range of additions. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffer capacity and added base]{ref}.

For the pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, rearranging the <acid dissociation constant> gives the activity relation
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
With <thermodynamic activity> of <water> approximated by one and ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities> for both partners, this yields the familiar <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right).
$$
The <concentrations> are at <chemical equilibrium>; using initial values requires negligible equilibration changes. Below, also neglect <self-ionization of water> and competing <chemical reactions>.

Stipulate $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$ for an illustrative <weak acid>, without identifying it with a measured substance. A <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio of two gives
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx4.76+\log_{10}2=5.061029995\ldots\approx5.06103.
$$
Equal <concentrations> give a ratio of one, whose <logarithm> is zero, hence <pH> $4.76$. Multiplying the ratio by ten raises <pH> by one: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give <pH> values $3.76$, $4.76$, and $5.76$. The ratio controls the ideal prediction, while the absolute quantities control how much added <chemical acid> or <chemical base> the <buffer solution> can accommodate.

\Image[buffer-ratio-ph.png]
{title=The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> turns a tenfold ratio change into one unit of <pH>}
{description=The stipulated <weak acid> has $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$. Points mark <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratios $0.1$, $1$, $2$, and $10$. The curve uses ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities>, <chemical equilibrium>, and negligible <self-ionization of water>; it is a calculation, rather than measured data.}

= Henderson-Hasselbalch equation
{c}
{parent=Buffer solution}
{wiki=Henderson–Hasselbalch_equation}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}([\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}])$}

The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> estimates <buffer solution> <pH> using a <weak acid>'s <acid dissociation constant> and the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio.

Its activity form is an exact rearrangement of the chosen <chemical equilibrium> relation. Write the acid dissociation step as $\mathrm{HA}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H^++\mathrm A^-$, with hydrated <hydrogen ions> understood in <water> and the <solvent> convention incorporated into the <acid dissociation constant>. For consistent <standard states>,
$$
K_a=\frac{a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}},\qquad
\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a,\qquad
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Here each $a_i$ is a <dimensionless> <thermodynamic activity> at <chemical equilibrium>. Exactness refers to this specified <acid dissociation constant> relation and the chosen single-<ion> <thermodynamic activity> convention, not to identifying the ratio with quantities initially mixed. If <water> is instead written explicitly as a <reactant>, its <thermodynamic activity> must be treated consistently. See \a[https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf][Hippler and Metcalfe, Using activities to correct the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation (2020), equations 3–4]{ref}.

For a toy <weak acid> with stipulated <pKa> $5$, use the ideal dilute <concentration> approximation and put $r=[\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}]$. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> becomes $\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}r$:
$$
r=0.1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx4,\qquad
r=1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx5,\qquad
r=10\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx6.
$$
More <conjugate base> relative to <weak acid> means higher <pH>. Each tenfold increase in the ratio contributes exactly one to the calculated <pH>. The stipulated <pKa> is not a tabulated value for an identified substance.

\Image[buffer-ph-versus-species-ratio.png]
{title=Toy <pKa> $5$: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give calculated <pH> $4$, $5$, and $6$}
{description=Both panels show the same <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> in the ideal <concentration> approximation, with linear and <logarithmic scales> for the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> ratio. The <logarithmic scale> turns the curve into a straight line. The plotted range is illustrative, rather than a guarantee of useful <buffer capacity>.}

The distinction between <thermodynamic activity> and <concentration> is explicit when $a_i=\gamma_i[i]/c^\circ$, where $\gamma_i$ is an <activity coefficient> and $c^\circ$ is the reference <concentration>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right)+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm A^-}}{\gamma_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Neglecting the final term gives the usual approximate <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>. It is justified when the ratio of <activity coefficients> is sufficiently close to one, as in a suitable dilute ideal approximation. <Ionic strength>, including contributions from other dissolved <ions>, affects <activity coefficients>; a nominally dilute <weak acid> alone does not establish that all corrections are small. The ratio must use <chemical equilibrium> <concentrations>. Replacing these by prepared amounts or post-<neutralization> inventories is a further approximation, suitable only when subsequent redistribution is small. See \a[https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf][Hippler and Metcalfe, concentration, activity, and analytical-concentration approximations]{ref}.

The short derivation is to solve the <acid dissociation constant> relation for $a_{\mathrm H^+}$, take a negative base-ten <logarithm>, and use the definitions of <pH> and <pKa>. Its useful beauty is that a <chemical equilibrium> becomes a simple rule about a ratio: select a <weak acid> with <pKa> near the desired <pH>, then adjust the partner ratio. Scaling both partner <concentrations> equally leaves the ideal <pH> prediction unchanged, while changing <buffer capacity>. In practical <buffer solutions>, <temperature>, <ionic strength>, and the available inventories still matter.

= Buffer capacity
{parent=Buffer solution}

<Buffer capacity> describes how much added <chemical acid> or <chemical base> a specified volume of <buffer solution> can accommodate for a specified change in <pH>. It depends on the total <concentration> and the balance of the conjugate partners; <pH> alone does not determine it. At the same initial partner ratio and volume, larger inventories generally permit a larger addition before the same <pH> change in the ideal model. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffer capacity]{ref}.

= Neutralization
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Neutralization_(chemistry)}

A <neutralization> is a <chemical reaction> between a <chemical acid> and a <chemical base>. In the <buffer solution> example, <hydroxide> takes a <proton> from the <weak acid> to form <water> and the <conjugate base>. The <stoichiometry> updates the partner inventories before the approximate <chemical equilibrium> calculation. The name does not require the resulting <pH> to be $7$.

= Mixture
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Solution
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Mixture}
{wiki}

= Chemical solution
{synonym}

A <chemical solution> is a homogeneous <mixture> in which designated <solutes> are distributed in a <solvent>. The distinction between <solute> and <solvent> is part of the description; <water> is the <solvent> in the aqueous <pH> examples.

= Ionic strength
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$}

The concentration-based <ionic strength> is $I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$, where $c_i$ is each dissolved <ion>'s <concentration> and $z_i$ its signed <electric charge> number. It has the same units as $c_i$; some formulas instead use the <dimensionless> ratio $I_c/c^\circ$. All dissolved <ions> contribute, including those that balance the <electric charge> of a <buffer solution>'s <conjugate base>. <Ionic strength> helps describe the interactions represented by <activity coefficients>. See \a[https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf][Hippler and Metcalfe, activity coefficients and dimensionless ionic strength]{ref}.

= Solute
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

A <solute> is a component of a <chemical solution> distinguished from its <solvent>. Its <concentration> describes how much is present relative to a specified quantity of <chemical solution> or <solvent>.

= Solvent
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}

= Dilution
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

<Dilution> lowers a <solute>'s <concentration> by adding <solvent>. When the <amount of substance> of that <solute> is conserved and $c$ denotes its amount per <volume (thermodynamics)> of <chemical solution>, $c_1V_1=c_2V_2$. This conservation statement does not by itself preserve the <pH>: a <chemical equilibrium> may change as the <chemical solution> is diluted.

= Stereochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Stereochemical
{synonym}

<Stereochemistry> studies the spatial arrangement of <chemical atoms> in <molecules>. A <Fischer projection> records this information at successive <stereocenters>, whereas a <molecular formula> only counts <chemical atoms>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-2-representing-carbohydrate-stereochemistry-fischer-projections][McMurry: representing carbohydrate stereochemistry]{ref}.

= Stereocenter
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

At a <stereocenter>, exchanging two attached groups produces a different spatial configuration. The open-chain <ribose> and <glucose> examples have <carbon> <stereocenters> bearing four different groups; C2 of <deoxyribose> has two identical <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/RS.html#stereogenic][Moss, Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: stereogenic units]{ref}.

= Fischer projection
{c}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

In a <Fischer projection>, each cross represents a <carbon> position: horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. For the open-chain <aldehyde> <sugars> illustrated here, place $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom. This is a convention for <stereochemistry>, not a measured molecular shape. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/FM.html#Fischer][Moss, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: Fischer projection]{ref}.

In these <Fischer projections>, D specifies that the <hydroxy group> at the lowest <stereocenter> lies on the right: C4 for <ribose> and <deoxyribose>, C5 for <glucose>. D does not specify every other <stereocenter>, and it does not specify the direction of rotation of polarized <light>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars][McMurry: the meaning of D and L]{ref}.

= Hydroxy group
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}$}

= Hydroxyl group
{synonym}

A <hydroxy group> is an $\mathrm{OH}$ group attached to the rest of a <molecule>. In the illustrated <sugars>, its <oxygen> is bonded to <carbon> and to <hydrogen>.

= Chemical substance
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrogen peroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2$}
{wiki}

= Acyl group
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}}$}

For the <carboxylic acid>-derived examples here, an <acyl group> is the group remaining after formal removal of $\mathrm{OH}$ from $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}OH}$. It includes the <oxygen> at the end of the <fatty acid>-derived chain, so it is more than just a <hydrocarbon> region. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/carba.html][IUPAC class names: carboxylic acids and derivatives]{ref}.

= Sphingosine
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Zwitterion
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Zwitterionic
{synonym}

A <zwitterion> has opposite formal <electric charges> in different regions of a <molecule>, with zero net <electric charge>. <Phosphatidylcholine> provides a membrane-related example. This is stronger than merely having <chemical polarity> through fractional <partial atomic charges>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/Z06752][IUPAC Gold Book: zwitterions]{ref}.

= Lipid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Lipids> are a chemically diverse family of <chemical substances>, conventionally associated with biological origin and solubility in <nonpolar> <solvents>. They include <triglycerides>, <phospholipids>, and <cholesterol>. The name does not specify one <molecular formula>, one repeating <monomer>, or one kind of <polymer>. Some <lipids> have both <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions rather than being uniformly <nonpolar>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03571][IUPAC Gold Book: lipids]{ref}.

Structure explains the different jobs. A <triglyceride> has three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> attached to <glycerol> and serves as an <energy> store. A typical <glycerophospholipid> instead combines two such chains with a <phosphate>-containing head, making it an <amphiphile> suited to a <lipid bilayer>. <Cholesterol> has a different structure and also contributes to <cell membrane> properties. Thus a <lipid> is a chemical building material, while a <lipid bilayer> is an organization of many <molecules>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.3: lipid structures and functions]{ref}.

= Triglyceride
{parent=Lipid}

= Triacylglycerol
{synonym}

A <triglyceride> contains <glycerol> joined to three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages. Compare the three chains with the two-chain, <phosphate>-headed <glycerophospholipid> illustrated below. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids][Biology 2e: fats and oils]{ref}.

= Cholesterol
{parent=Lipid}

<Cholesterol> is a <lipid> with four fused rings and a small <hydrophilic> region. It helps regulate the fluidity of animal <cell membranes>; it is structurally distinct from a <phospholipid>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids][Biology 2e: cholesterol]{ref}.

= Phospholipid
{parent=Lipid}
{wiki}

<Phospholipids> are <lipids> containing a <phosphate> group as part of their structure linked by <covalent bonds>. The familiar membrane-forming <phospholipids> are <amphiphilic>: a <hydrophilic> head region with <chemical polarity> coexists with a <hydrophobic>, largely <nonpolar> <hydrocarbon> region. These descriptions refer to different parts of the same <molecule>, not to two separate substances. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04559][IUPAC Gold Book: phospholipids]{ref} and \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303][IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic]{ref}.

A typical two-chain <glycerophospholipid> provides a useful example: a <glycerol> scaffold carries two <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages, plus a <phosphate>-containing head region. <Phosphatidylcholine> is one example. This scaffold describes an important subclass, rather than all <phospholipids>: <sphingomyelin> has a different backbone, and even <glycerophospholipids> need not all have two <acyl groups>. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html][IUPAC–IUB, Nomenclature of Lipids (1976), Lip-1.5 and Lip-2.1–2.6]{ref}.

\Image[phospholipid-amphiphilic-schematic.png]
{title=Polar head and <hydrophobic> chains of an illustrative <glycerophospholipid>}
{description=Colored regions represent a <phosphate>-containing head, a <glycerol> scaffold, and two <fatty acid>-derived chains. The small paired-chain symbol is shorthand for this illustrative subclass. Shapes and connectors show organization, not <chemical atoms>, exact <chemical bonds>, chain lengths, or a measured <molecular geometry>. The head is not assigned a universal <electric charge>.}

Polar does not mean negatively charged overall. At near-neutral <pH>, <phosphatidylcholine> is <zwitterionic>: its <phosphate> region carries negative <electric charge> and its <choline> region positive <electric charge>, with zero net <electric charge> for the <molecule>. Counting those opposite charges gives $(-1)+(+1)=0$; it does not remove their local interactions with <water>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/][Lodish et al., Molecular Cell Biology: membrane lipids]{ref}.

In a <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> orient their <hydrophilic> heads toward the <water> on either side and their <hydrophobic> chains toward the shared interior. The <hydrophobic effect> helps explain this organization through the <free energy> of the <water> and <lipids> together. It is not a special repulsive <chemical bond> between chains and <water>. Which organization is favored also depends on <molecular geometry>, <temperature>, <concentration>, and the surrounding <chemical solution>; a <phospholipid> label alone does not guarantee a <lipid bilayer>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer]{ref}.

\Image[phospholipid-bilayer-organization.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> shelter a <hydrophobic> interior between water-facing heads}
{description=An illustrative <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer> has <water> on both sides. Individual <phospholipids> can undergo <diffusion> within a fluid <membrane leaflet>. The heads and chains are schematic regions; spacing, arrows, and chain shapes are unscaled. This is a local cross-section, without assigning either side to a particular <biological cell> compartment.}

This organization supplies a flexible barrier for compartments. A fluid <lipid bilayer> allows <lipids> to undergo <diffusion> within each <membrane leaflet>; barrier function therefore coexists with motion of <molecules>, as in the <fluid mosaic model>. It is not completely impermeable: small <nonpolar> <molecules> such as <molecular oxygen> and <carbon dioxide> cross much more readily than <ions>. In a <cell membrane>, <ion channels> and other <membrane transport proteins> provide selective routes; the existing <membrane transport> article explains their driving forces. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/][Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport]{ref}.

In 1925, E. Gorter and F. Grendel extracted <lipids> from <red blood cells> and compared the <surface area> of a one-<molecule>-thick film on <water> with the estimated total <surface area> of the <red blood cells>. Their table gave ratios near two, supporting a two-layer covering; they also proposed heads facing the aqueous surroundings on both sides. The inference depends on recovering the relevant <lipids>, estimating the <red blood cell> <surface area> correctly, and comparing compatible packing in the film and the <cell membrane>. It was indirect evidence, not an image of the <lipid bilayer>. The paper itself notes possible losses during evaporation. See \a[https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.41.4.439][Gorter and Grendel, On Bimolecular Layers of Lipoids on the Chromocytes of the Blood, Journal of Experimental Medicine 41 (1925), 439–443]{ref}.

= Glycerophospholipid
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Glycerophospholipids> are <phospholipids> built on a <glycerol>-<phosphate> scaffold. The illustrated two-<acyl group> form is common, but the subclass also includes other substitutions and one-chain derivatives. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html][IUPAC–IUB lipid nomenclature, Lip-2.2 and Lip-2.6]{ref}.

= Phosphatidylcholine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}
{wiki}

= PC
{c}
{synonym}

<Phosphatidylcholine>, abbreviated <PC>, is a <glycerophospholipid> with a <phosphate>-<choline> head. Its <zwitterionic> head illustrates why a <hydrophilic> region need not have a nonzero net <electric charge>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/][Lodish et al.: membrane lipids]{ref}.

= Sphingomyelin
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Sphingomyelin> is a <phospholipid> whose backbone is based on <sphingosine>, rather than <glycerol>. One long <hydrocarbon> region belongs to that backbone and another comes from an attached <fatty acid>. It is a counterexample to defining every <phospholipid> by the illustrated <glycerol> scaffold. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper, The Cell: the molecular composition of cells]{ref}.

= Aldehyde
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <aldehyde> has a group $\mathrm R\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)\mathrm H$, written $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top of the illustrated open-chain <Fischer projections>; $R$ denotes the attached remainder of the <molecule>.

= Carbohydrate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Carbohydrates> are a family of <chemical substances> encompassing <monosaccharides>, combinations of <monosaccharides>, and their derivatives. <Glucose> is a small <carbohydrate>; <cellulose> is a long-chain <polysaccharide>. <Deoxyribose> illustrates why composition alone does not define the family: replacing a <hydroxy group> in <ribose> by <hydrogen> still leaves a <carbohydrate>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820][IUPAC Gold Book: carbohydrates]{ref}.

Many familiar <monosaccharides> have a <molecular formula> of the form $\mathrm C_n\mathrm H_{2n}\mathrm O_n$, equivalently $\mathrm C_n(\mathrm H_2\mathrm O)_n$. This explains the historical name, “hydrate of <carbon>”, but does not mean the <molecule> consists of <carbon> mixed with intact <water> <molecules>. Nor is it a universal formula: <deoxyribose> has $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. The family is defined through chemical structure and relationships, not that ratio. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820][IUPAC's definition and origin of the name carbohydrate]{ref}.

The useful surprise is the variety of roles obtainable from related building blocks. <Glucose> participates in <metabolism>, <ribose> forms part of <RNA>, and linked <glucose> units make structural <cellulose> in a plant <cell wall>. Understanding <carbohydrates> therefore connects cellular <energy>, molecular information, and material structure. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

= Sucrose
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}}$}

<Sucrose> dissolves in <water> as neutral <molecules>; it is a <nonelectrolyte> in this <solvent>.

= Sugar
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

In chemical usage, <sugars> commonly means small <carbohydrates>, including single-unit <monosaccharides> and two-unit <disaccharides>. <Glucose>, <ribose>, and <deoxyribose> are <monosaccharides>. <Cellulose> is instead a <polysaccharide> assembled from many <glucose> units. The distinction counts chemically linked units; it does not classify every <carbohydrate> by sweetness or biological role. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.2: carbohydrates]{ref}.

A useful first description of a <monosaccharide> asks how many <carbon> <chemical atoms> it contains and what its open-chain structure is. <Ribose> is an <aldopentose>, a five-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugar>; <glucose> is an <aldohexose>, with six <carbon> <chemical atoms>. A <Fischer projection> then records the <stereochemistry>, information missing from the <molecular formula>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates][McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.1: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Ribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$}

<Ribose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. Its biologically familiar form is D-ribose, an <aldopentose> whose open-chain <Fischer projection> has $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top, $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom, and <hydroxy groups> on the right at C2, C3, and C4. The opposite horizontal substituents are <hydrogen>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars][McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.3: D-ribose and D-glucose configurations]{ref}.

\Image[pentose-sugar-fischer-comparison.png]
{title=Open-chain <Fischer projections> of D-<ribose> and 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>}
{description=At each cross, horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. Both drawings number the free <sugar> from its <aldehyde> carbon, C1. In <deoxyribose>, C2 has two <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. These are open-chain reference structures, not a depiction of the predominant <chemical species> in <water>.}

Read the drawing by counting groups: $\mathrm{CHO}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H\mathrm O$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups contribute $\mathrm C_3\mathrm H_6\mathrm O_3$, and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H_3\mathrm O$. Their sum is $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. The count checks composition; the right/right/right pattern additionally specifies the <stereochemistry> of D-<ribose>.

In <RNA>, <ribose> occurs as a ring within each <nucleotide>, joining a <nucleobase> to the <phosphate>-linked backbone. Its C2 position retains a <hydroxy group>, unlike <deoxyribose> in <DNA>. In a <nucleotide>, the <sugar> positions carry primes, so this is C2′. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al.: nucleotide sugars in RNA and DNA]{ref}. Free <ribose> in <water> is also predominantly cyclic, with several ring forms in <chemical equilibrium> and very little open-chain form; the <Fischer projection> is valuable for comparing configurations, not for estimating their abundances. See \a[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3819725/][Mason et al., The Conformation of a Ribose Derivative in Aqueous Solution: A Neutron Scattering and Molecular Dynamics Study (2013), introduction]{ref}.

= Deoxyribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$}

In the usual <DNA> context, <deoxyribose> means 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>, a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. Relative to D-<ribose>, the <hydroxy group> at C2 is replaced by <hydrogen>. The locant 2 matters: it identifies which position loses its <oxygen>. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html#P-102.5.3][Favre and Powell, IUPAC Blue Book, P-102.5.3: deoxy sugars]{ref} and \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/nucle.html][IUPAC: nucleic acids and their component sugars]{ref}.

The paired <Fischer projections> in <ribose> make the change concrete. C2 has <hydrogen> on both sides, while C3 and C4 retain their <hydroxy groups> on the right. C2 is therefore not a <stereocenter>: exchanging its two identical <hydrogen> substituents cannot produce a distinct configuration. The unchanged C3 and C4 still carry <stereochemical> information.

Replacing $\mathrm{OH}$ by $\mathrm H$ removes one <oxygen> but leaves the total <hydrogen> count unchanged: the <hydrogen> belonging to the removed <hydroxy group> is replaced by a <hydrogen> bonded directly to <carbon>. Thus $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$ becomes $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. This comparison describes structures, rather than a complete <chemical reaction> or its mechanism.

In <DNA>, <deoxyribose> is incorporated as a ring in each <nucleotide>, with <phosphate> connections building the backbone. Its C2′ bears <hydrogen> where <RNA>'s <ribose> bears a <hydroxy group>. This small local difference lets the <sugar> structure identify the two kinds of <nucleic acid>; the <nucleobases> supply their sequence information. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al.: nucleotides as the subunits of DNA and RNA]{ref}.

= Glucose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6$}

<Glucose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. Its familiar biological form, D-glucose, is an <aldohexose>. In the open-chain <Fischer projection>, $\mathrm{CHO}$ is at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom; the <hydroxy groups> at C2, C3, C4, and C5 point right, left, right, right. This full pattern distinguishes D-<glucose> from other six-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugars>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-4-configurations-of-the-aldoses][McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.4: configurations of the aldoses]{ref}.

\Image[glucose-fischer-projection.png]
{title=D-<glucose>: six <carbon> positions and four open-chain <stereocenters>}
{description=Horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer; vertical <chemical bonds> point away. The <hydroxy group> pattern from C2 through C5 is right/left/right/right. $\mathrm{CHO}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ are condensed end groups. The diagram records an open-chain reference structure, not the predominant form in an aqueous <chemical solution>.}

The <molecular formula> can be checked directly: one $\mathrm{CHO}$ group, four $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups, and one $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ group give six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. All four interior <carbon> positions are <stereocenters> in this open-chain structure. Neither their orientations nor the arrangement of <chemical bonds> can be recovered from the <molecular formula> alone.

In <water>, <glucose> exists mainly in six-membered rings in <chemical equilibrium> with a small amount of open-chain <glucose>. Ring closure connects the <hydroxy group> at C5 to the <aldehyde> carbon at C1; the ring contains five <carbon> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen>, with C6 outside it. No <chemical atoms> are lost, so the <molecular formula> remains $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-5-cyclic-structures-of-monosaccharides-anomers][McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.5: cyclic structures of monosaccharides]{ref}.

<Glucose> is useful both as a substrate in cellular <metabolism> and as a building block of <polysaccharides>. <Cellulose> chains assembled from <glucose> units help support plant <cell walls>. The same small <sugar> thus connects chemical fuel to a structural material; its role depends on how it is transformed or linked. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper, The Cell: A Molecular Approach, the molecular composition of cells]{ref}.

= Monosaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <monosaccharide> is a single-unit <carbohydrate> that cannot be split into smaller <sugars> by <hydrolysis>. <Ribose>, <deoxyribose>, and <glucose> are examples. “Single-unit” does not mean a single <chemical atom> or a permanently open chain. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates][McMurry: simple carbohydrates]{ref}.

= Aldopentose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldopentose> is a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Ribose> is an example; <deoxyribose> is its 2-deoxy derivative. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates][McMurry: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Aldohexose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldohexose> is a six-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Glucose> is an example. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates][McMurry: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Disaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <disaccharide> contains two linked <monosaccharide> units. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates][Biology 2e: disaccharides]{ref}.

= Polysaccharide
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

A <polysaccharide> is a <polymer> built from many linked <monosaccharide> units. <Cellulose> provides a structural example. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates][Biology 2e: polysaccharides]{ref}.

= Cellulose
{parent=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Cellulose> is a <polysaccharide> of <glucose> units whose chains form strong assemblies in plant <cell walls>. It illustrates a structural role for <carbohydrates>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26928/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the plant cell wall]{ref}.

= Triose phosphate
{parent=Carbohydrate}

A <triose phosphate> is a three-<carbon> <sugar> carrying a <phosphate> group. <Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate> is one example. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net <triose phosphate> for further <carbohydrate> synthesis. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: the Calvin cycle's carbohydrate product]{ref}.

= Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate
{parent=Triose phosphate}
{wiki}

= G3P
{c}
{synonym}

= Phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Inorganic phosphate
{parent=Phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm P_i$}

<Inorganic phosphate>, abbreviated $\mathrm P_i$, denotes the free <phosphate> pool in biochemical <chemical equations>, including <ATP> <hydrolysis>. Its constituent <chemical species> depend on <pH> and <protonation>; $\mathrm P_i$ does not specify a single <electric charge> or proton count. See the \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/][IUBMB recommendations on biochemical reactant pools]{ref}.

= Chemical formula
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical formula> uses symbols of <chemical elements> and numerical subscripts to record composition. In the <molecular formula> $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ of <water>, the subscript $2$ counts two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and the omitted subscript after $\mathrm O$ means one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. A <molecular formula> describes one <molecule>; a <chemical formula> need not describe a discrete <molecule>.

Read a preceding coefficient separately from the subscripts. One $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains two <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>; $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> distributed between two <water> <molecules>. In $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$, the second subscript instead puts two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> into each <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Thus $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\ne\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$: the coefficient scales the whole <chemical formula>, while a subscript specifies its composition. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-4-chemical-formulas][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.4: chemical formulas]{ref}.

\Image[chemical-formula-and-coefficient.png]
{title=A coefficient counts whole <molecules>; a subscript counts <chemical atoms> within each <molecule>}
{description=The three columns show one <water> <molecule>, two <water> <molecules>, and one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Their <hydrogen> counts are $2,4,2$ and their <oxygen> counts are $1,2,2$. Connections are schematic $\mathrm{H-O-H}$ and $\mathrm{H-O-O-H}$, not models of actual <bond angles> or <bond lengths>.}

For crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, the <chemical formula> specifies a $1:1$ ratio of <sodium> <ions> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ to <chlorine> <ions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$. Its <formula unit> is the accounting entity; the ordinary crystal is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than a collection of discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. Specifying the entity matters when assigning an <amount of substance>. See <formula unit> and \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-6-ionic-and-molecular-compounds][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.6: ionic and molecular compounds]{ref}.

A <molecular formula> gives an atom inventory, not the full arrangement of <chemical bonds> or the <molecular geometry>. For example, $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$ counts six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in a <glucose> <molecule>, but does not specify the pattern shown by its <Fischer projection>. Different structures can share a <molecular formula>. This compact inventory is useful for <chemical equation> accounting precisely because it leaves the structural detail to other representations.

The choice of letters has an early explanation in \a[https://web2.lemoyne.edu/~giunta/berzelius.html][Jöns Jacob Berzelius, Essay on the Cause of Chemical Proportions (1813–1814), §III, transcribed by Carmen Giunta]{ref}: letters made chemical signs easier to write and print, and formulas compressed information about composition. His numerical notation differed from modern subscripts; the lasting idea was to make proportions readable in ordinary text.

= Molecular formula
{parent=Chemical formula}

A molecular formula gives the number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> in a <molecule>. For <urea>, $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ counts one <carbon>, four <hydrogen>, two <nitrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. It records composition rather than the arrangement of <chemical bonds>.

= Formula unit
{parent=Chemical formula}
{wiki}

A <formula unit> is a specified compositional accounting entity for a <chemical substance> that need not consist of discrete <molecules>. For ordinary crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, one <formula unit> counts one <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and one <chlorine> <ion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$; it does not identify an isolated pair within the crystal. \a[https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf#page=65][Cohen et al., IUPAC Green Book, 3rd ed., §2.10.1(iii)(b), p. 51]{ref} defines a <formula unit> as a specified group of <chemical atoms>, rather than a unit of measurement. An <amount of substance> of one <mole> of $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <formula units> therefore corresponds to one <mole> of each kind of <ion>.

= Organic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Carboxylic acid
{parent=Organic acid}
{wiki}

= Acetic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$}

<Acetic acid> is a <weak acid> that partly transfers <protons> to <water>, supplying <hydronium> and negative <ions>. It is therefore a <weak electrolyte> in <water>.

= Fatty acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

A <fatty acid> is a <carboxylic acid> with a <hydrocarbon> chain. Its chain supplies much of the <hydrophobic> region when incorporated into a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids][Biology 2e: fatty acids]{ref}.

= Amino acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}

= Methionine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Met},\ \mathrm M$}

Methionine is an <amino acid> represented by Met or M in <protein> sequence notation. In the standard <genetic code>, AUG encodes it, as in the worked <gene expression> example. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code][OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: the standard genetic code]{ref}

= Alanine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ala},\ \mathrm A$}

Alanine is an <amino acid> represented by Ala or A in <protein> sequence notation. GCU is one of its <codons> in the standard <genetic code>, and supplies the second residue in the worked <gene expression> example. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code][OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: alanine codons]{ref}

= Citric acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_8\mathrm O_7$}

Its <chemical formula> and structure are recorded in \a[https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:30769][ChEBI, citric acid]{ref}.

= Citrate
{parent=Citric acid}

Citrate denotes an <ion> derived from <citric acid>. The commonly specified form has <electric charge> equal to that of three <electrons> and <chemical formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm O_7^{3-}$; it results from removing three <hydrogen> <ions> from <citric acid>. Forms with additional <hydrogen> <ions> attached through <protonation> also occur. \a[https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:16947][ChEBI, citrate(3−)]{ref}

= Hydrogen deuteride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{HD}$}

The diatomic molecule containing one ordinary hydrogen nucleus and one deuterium nucleus is an isotopic form of <molecular hydrogen>. It has a weak electric dipole and can supply low-temperature <molecular line cooling> in primordial gas when its abundance is sufficient.

= Chemical element
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical element> is an identity shared by all <chemical atoms> whose <atomic nuclei> contain the same number of <protons>. That number is its <atomic number>, $Z$: six means <carbon>, eleven means <sodium>. The identity survives differences in <neutron> count, which distinguish <isotopes>, and differences in <electron> count, which distinguish neutral <chemical atoms> from <ions>. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01022][IUPAC Gold Book, chemical element]{ref}.

The same name can also denote a pure <chemical substance> containing only that <chemical element>. These two uses answer different questions. <Molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, is a <chemical substance> made entirely of <oxygen>; <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, is a <chemical compound> containing both <hydrogen> and <oxygen>. A <water> <molecule> contains three <chemical atoms> but only two <chemical elements>. Joining <chemical atoms> through <chemical bonds> changes their arrangement without changing their <atomic numbers>.

This distinction makes <stoichiometry> possible: ordinary <chemical reactions> rearrange <chemical atoms>, so one can count each <chemical element> before and after a transformation. Changing the <proton> count instead changes the <chemical element> and requires a <nuclear reaction>. The <periodic table> organizes these identities into a useful map, while <atomic number> bookkeeping keeps identity, <isotope>, and <electric charge> separate.

= Periodic table
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

The <periodic table> arranges <chemical elements> in increasing <atomic number>, across successive <periodic table periods>. Its columns are <periodic groups>, numbered 1–18. The layout places recurring outer <electron configurations> above one another, making similarities in <chemical bonds> and <chemical reactions> easier to recognize. Ordering is by <proton> count, not by <mass>; the <mass number> of a particular <isotope> does not determine its box. See the \a[https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/][official IUPAC periodic table and numbering conventions]{ref}.

For the first eighteen neutral <chemical atoms> in their <ground states>, a compact description counts <electrons> in successive <electron shells>. Thus <carbon> has $(2,4)$, meaning two <electrons> in the first <electron shell> and four in the second; the sum $2+4=6$ agrees with its <atomic number>. <Sodium> has $(2,8,1)$, whose sum is $11$. These are occupancy counts, not circular trajectories of <electrons>.

\Image[first-three-periods.png]
{title=The first three <periodic table periods>: <atomic numbers> and neutral <ground state> <electron shell> occupancies}
{description=Colours distinguish <periodic group> columns only, without encoding numerical property trends. The spaces in columns 3–12 preserve the eighteen-column <periodic table> layout: no <chemical elements> occupy those positions in these three <periodic table periods>. Each cell gives a <chemical element>'s symbol, <atomic number>, and <electron shell> counts.}

Reading each row from left to right gives:
* <Periodic table period> 1: <hydrogen> $(1)$ and <helium> $(2)$.
* <Periodic table period> 2: <lithium> $(2,1)$, <beryllium> $(2,2)$, <boron> $(2,3)$, <carbon> $(2,4)$, <nitrogen> $(2,5)$, <oxygen> $(2,6)$, <fluorine> $(2,7)$, and <neon> $(2,8)$.
* <Periodic table period> 3: <sodium> $(2,8,1)$, <magnesium> $(2,8,2)$, <aluminium> $(2,8,3)$, <silicon> $(2,8,4)$, <phosphorus> $(2,8,5)$, <sulfur> $(2,8,6)$, <chlorine> $(2,8,7)$, and <argon> $(2,8,8)$.

The counting outline is short: the occupied <electron subshells> in this range are $1s$, $2s$, $2p$, $3s$, and $3p$, filled in that order for these neutral <ground states>. Each $s$ <electron subshell> holds up to two <electrons> and each $p$ <electron subshell> up to six, giving $2$, $2+6=8$, and $2+6=8$ along these three rows. This $2,8,8$ summary applies only through <argon>; it is not a general rule that each <electron shell> fills completely before the next begins, nor a claim that the third <electron shell> can hold only eight <electrons>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electronic structure and electron configurations]{ref}.

The recurring outer counts make the arrangement useful. <Lithium> and <sodium> each have one <valence electron>, whereas <fluorine> and <chlorine> each have seven <valence electrons>. Such similarities help anticipate <chemical reactions>, without making all properties identical throughout a <periodic group>. <Helium> is an instructive exception to an eight-<electron> shortcut: its two <electrons> complete the first <electron shell>, so it belongs above <neon> and <argon> in <periodic group> 18. <Hydrogen> shares column 1 with <lithium> and <sodium> but has its own distinct chemistry. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-5-the-periodic-table][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, periodic-table organization]{ref}.

= Group
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic group
{synonym}

= Periodic table group
{synonym}

A <periodic group> is a vertical column of the <periodic table>. For example, <fluorine> and <chlorine> are in <periodic group> 17, with seven <valence electrons> in each neutral <ground state> <chemical atom>. A shared outer <electron configuration> supports similarities in <chemical reactions>, rather than equality of every property.

= Period
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic table period
{synonym}

A <periodic table period> is a horizontal row of the <periodic table>, read in increasing <atomic number>. <Periodic table period> 2 runs from <lithium>, $Z=3$, to <neon>, $Z=10$: $10-3+1=8$ <chemical elements>. In these neutral <ground states>, the second <electron shell> gains <electrons> while the first retains two.

= Boron
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm B$, $Z=5$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> five.

= Fluorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm F$, $Z=9$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> nine.

= Aluminium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}$, $Z=13$}
{wiki}

= Aluminum
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> thirteen.

= Phosphorus
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm P$, $Z=15$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> fifteen.

= Sulfur
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm S$, $Z=16$}
{wiki}

= Sulphur
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> sixteen.

= Chlorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl}$, $Z=17$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> seventeen.

= Argon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ar}$, $Z=18$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> eighteen.

= Sodium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Na}$}

Sodium is the <chemical element> of <atomic number> eleven. Carbon-fusion channels in <carbon burning> include ${}^{12}\mathrm C+{}^{12}\mathrm C\longrightarrow{}^{23}\mathrm{Na}+p$, producing sodium-23 and a <proton>. Its abundance in expelled stellar material depends on later reactions and mixing, rather than on this one production channel alone.

= Calcium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}$}
{wiki}

= Potassium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{K}$}
{wiki}

= Atomic number
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$Z$}
{wiki}

The <atomic number> $Z$ is the number of <protons> in an <atomic nucleus>, and fixes the <chemical element>. It is a count, with no units. An <atomic nucleus> with six <protons> is always <carbon>, regardless of how many <neutrons> accompany them or how many <electrons> surround it. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00499][IUPAC Gold Book, atomic number]{ref}.

Three independent bookkeeping questions become easy once their symbols are kept separate. Let $N$ count <neutrons>, $n_e$ count <electrons>, $A$ be the <mass number>, and $Q$ be the net <electric charge>. A <proton> carries <electric charge> $+e$ and an <electron> carries $-e$, where $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>; a <neutron> carries zero net <electric charge>. Hence
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z,\qquad Q=(Z-n_e)e,\qquad n_e=Z-Q/e.
$$
The reasoning is just counting: add <protons> and <neutrons> for $A$, and subtract negative <electron> contributions from positive <proton> contributions for $Q$. A neutral <chemical atom> has $Q=0$, so $n_e=Z$. For a positively charged <ion>, fewer <electrons> remain. Notation ${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$ gives the <mass number> at upper left and <atomic number> at lower left, with $\mathrm X$ standing for the <chemical element>; a right superscript such as $+$ specifies the <electric charge> in units of $e$. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, atomic structure and symbolism]{ref}.

For neutral <carbon-12>, ${}^{12}_{6}\mathrm C$, the <atomic number> gives six <protons>, the subtraction $N=12-6=6$ gives six <neutrons>, and neutrality gives six <electrons>. Neutral <carbon-13>, ${}^{13}_{6}\mathrm C$, instead has $N=13-6=7$ <neutrons>, with the same six <protons> and six <electrons>. Thus these two <isotopes> occupy the same <carbon> box in the <periodic table>.

For neutral <sodium> of <mass number> $23$, ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}$, the counts are eleven <protons>, $23-11=12$ <neutrons>, and eleven <electrons>. Removing one <electron> gives the <ion> ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}^{+}$: $Q/e=1$, so $n_e=11-1=10$. Both its <atomic number> and its twelve <neutrons> stay unchanged. This is <ionization>, not conversion into the <chemical element> <neon>, even though a neutral <neon> <chemical atom> also has ten <electrons>.

\Image[atomic-number-isotopes-and-ions.png]
{title=<Atomic number> fixes <chemical element> identity: changing <isotopes> changes <neutrons>, while forming a <sodium> <ion> changes <electrons>}
{description=The dots are counting tokens, with <protons> and <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus> inventory and <electrons> in a separate inventory. Their layout does not depict physical positions, sizes, or orbits. Neutral <carbon-12> and <carbon-13> differ by one <neutron>; neutral <sodium> of <mass number> 23 and its singly positive <ion> differ by one <electron>.}

This bookkeeping is useful whenever a symbol must be translated into a physical inventory. <Mass number> answers how many <protons> plus <neutrons> there are; <electric charge> answers the imbalance between <protons> and <electrons>; <atomic number> alone answers which <chemical element> it is. Keeping those questions distinct prevents an <isotope> or an <ion> from being mistaken for a different <chemical element>.

= Beryllium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Be}$}
{wiki}

Beryllium is the <chemical element> with atomic number four. Its isotope <beryllium-7> provides an important route to primordial <lithium-7>.

= Beryllium-7
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Be}$}

Beryllium-7 contains four <protons> and three <neutrons>. It forms in reactions including $^3\mathrm{He}+{}^4\mathrm{He}\to{}^7\mathrm{Be}+\gamma$ and becomes <lithium-7> through <electron capture>. In the early universe its ionization state affects when capture occurs, so the final primordial lithium inventory includes the earlier beryllium inventory rather than just lithium present when nuclear burning ends.

= Lithium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li}$}
{wiki}

Lithium is the <chemical element> with atomic number three. Its stable isotopes include lithium-6 and <lithium-7>. Lithium observed in old stellar atmospheres is an important but processing-sensitive test of <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Lithium-7
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Li}$}

Lithium-7 has three <protons> and four <neutrons>. In <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> it can form directly or through <beryllium-7>, which later undergoes <electron capture>. Competition between direct lithium production, lithium destruction and beryllium production produces a valley in the primordial lithium abundance versus <baryon-to-photon ratio>. Its measured stellar surface abundance need not equal its birth abundance because stellar transport and nuclear burning can remove lithium.

= Isotope
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

Isotopes are atoms of the same <chemical element>, with the same number of <protons> but different numbers of <neutrons>. Their abundance ratios can distinguish material sources and biological or chemical fractionation, while radioactive isotopes can additionally carry chronological information.

= Mass number
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$A=Z+N$}
{wiki}

The <mass number> $A$ counts <protons> plus <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus>. It is a whole-number count, rather than a measured <mass>. Subtracting the <atomic number> gives the <neutron> count, $N=A-Z$. See the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/M03726][IUPAC Gold Book, mass number]{ref}.

= Uranium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{U}$, $Z=92$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 92. Its long-lived radioactive isotopes illustrate actinide production by the <rapid neutron-capture process>.

= Lead
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Pb}$, $Z=82$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 82. Lead isotopes are important heavy products of the <slow neutron-capture process> in suitable neutron-exposure conditions.

= Barium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ba}$, $Z=56$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 56. Enrichment in barium can reveal transferred <slow neutron-capture process> products, as in a <barium star>.

= Silicon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Si}$, $Z=14$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 14. <Oxygen burning> produces silicon-group nuclei; subsequent <silicon burning> is a reaction network approaching an iron-group mixture.

= Magnesium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mg}$, $Z=12$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 12. Magnesium isotopes occur among products of <carbon burning> and alpha-capture neutron-source reactions.

= Neon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ne}$, $Z=10$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 10. <Neon burning> uses photodisintegration and alpha capture, while neon-22 can provide neutrons through $^{22}\mathrm{Ne}(\alpha,n){}^{25}\mathrm{Mg}$.

= Cobalt
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Co}$, $Z=27$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 27. Cobalt-56 is the intermediate radioactive nucleus in the <nickel-56 decay chain>.

= Nickel
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ni}$, $Z=28$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 28. Radioactive nickel-56 is produced in explosive burning; the <nickel-56 decay chain> contributes energy to supernova emission.

= Nickel-56 decay chain
{parent=Nickel}
{title2=$^{56}\mathrm{Ni}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Co}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Fe}$}

The radioactive chain converts nickel-56 through cobalt-56 into iron-56, releasing energy that can power supernova light curves. <Electron capture> and, where allowed, <beta-plus decay> convert protons into neutrons; gamma rays and charged decay products deposit some of the released energy. Ionization and escape of decay radiation affect the observed heating.

= Iron
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe}$, $Z=26$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 26. Iron-group <nuclear binding energy> and <silicon burning> are central to the termination of ordinary energy-producing stellar fusion.

= Oxygen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

= Oxygen-16
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$^{16}\mathrm O$}

= Molecular oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O_2$}

The diatomic form of <oxygen>. Modern terrestrial abundance is maintained largely by oxygenic <photosynthesis>, balanced by chemical and biological sinks.

= Nitrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm N$}
{wiki}

= Carbon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-12
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{12}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-13
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{13}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

The <carbon> <isotope> with six <protons> and seven <neutrons>. A neutral <carbon-13> <chemical atom> has six <electrons>.

= Helium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

= Helium-3
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^3\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

This stable <helium> isotope contains two <protons> and one <neutron>. In solar proton-proton burning it is an intermediate: two such nuclei can form <helium-4> and return two <protons>, or one can capture <helium-4> to enter the second chain branch. The balance between production and destruction determines the <helium-3 equilibrium abundance> and its <helium-3 relaxation time>.

= Helium-4
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^4\mathrm{He}$}

Helium-4 is the stable helium isotope with two protons and two neutrons; its nucleus is an alpha particle. Its strong binding makes it the principal neutron-containing product of primordial <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Hydrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

= Metallic hydrogen
{parent=Hydrogen}
{wiki}

Metallic hydrogen is a high-pressure conducting state of <hydrogen>. Its fluid form in the deep interiors of <gas giants> supplies a conducting medium for a <planetary dynamo>, unlike the iron-alloy conducting cores of <terrestrial planets>.

= Deuterium
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$^2\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

This stable <hydrogen> isotope has one <proton> and one <neutron>. In the <Proton–proton chain> it is produced by the slow weak proton-pair reaction and is rapidly consumed by <proton> capture to make <helium-3>. Its short nuclear lifetime inside a stellar core allows a <deuterium quasi-equilibrium in proton-proton burning> approximation.

= Hydrogen anion
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$\mathrm H^-$}
{wiki}

= Negative hydrogen ion
{synonym}

A negative hydrogen ion is a <hydrogen> nucleus with two bound electrons. Its weakly bound extra electron permits absorption at visible and infrared wavelengths in cool stellar atmospheres.

= Molecular nitrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{N}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Molecular hydrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Chemical compound
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Sodium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaCl}$}

<Sodium chloride> contains <sodium> <cations> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and <chlorine> <anions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ in a $1:1$ ratio; its <formula unit> has zero net <electric charge>.

= Glycerol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_3\mathrm{H}_8\mathrm{O}_3$}

<Glycerol> has three <carbon> <chemical atoms> and three $\mathrm{OH}$ groups. It supplies the scaffold of <triglycerides> and <glycerophospholipids>, but is not itself a long <hydrocarbon> chain. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids][Biology 2e: glycerol]{ref}.

= Hydrocarbon
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

A <hydrocarbon> contains only <carbon> and <hydrogen>. A <fatty acid> also contains <oxygen>; calling its long chain a <hydrocarbon> region does not make the whole <fatty acid> a <hydrocarbon>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02889][IUPAC Gold Book: hydrocarbons]{ref}.

= Ester
{parent=Chemical compound}

The <carboxylic acid>-derived <ester> linkages in a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid> have the form $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}O{-}R'}$. Here the <acyl group> is attached through <oxygen> to the <glycerol>-derived part. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P6.html][IUPAC Blue Book, P-65.6: esters]{ref}.

= Choline
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Disulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Urea
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$}
{wiki}

Urea is the <chemical compound> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ and structure $(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$. See the \a[https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C57136&Mask=20][NIST Chemistry WebBook, urea: formula and structure]{ref}. Concentrated urea is used to promote <protein denaturation> in the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= 2-Mercaptoethanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$}
{wiki}

= Mercaptoethanol
{synonym}

2-Mercaptoethanol is the <chemical compound> $\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$; see the \a[https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C60242&Units=SI][NIST Chemistry WebBook, 2-mercaptoethanol: structure and names]{ref}. In the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>, it breaks <protein disulfide bonds>, while the <peptide bonds> of the <protein backbone> remain intact. This separates a change in crosslinks from cutting the <polypeptide chain>.

= Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

<Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate> is a <cofactor> used in <metabolism>. Its oxidized and reduced forms, <NADP+> and <NADPH>, carry <electrons> between coupled <chemical reactions>. In <oxygenic photosynthesis>, <light-dependent reactions> produce <NADPH> for the <Calvin cycle>, which returns <NADP+> for reuse. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling NADP+ and NADPH]{ref}.

= NADP+
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$}

= Oxidized NADP
{synonym}

<NADP+> is the oxidized form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It can accept <electrons> to form <NADPH>; the <light-dependent reactions> supply this conversion during <oxygenic photosynthesis>.

= NADPH
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADPH}$}

<NADPH> is the reduced form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It carries <electrons> used in synthesis; in the <Calvin cycle> it becomes the oxidized form <NADP+>, available for reuse by <light-dependent reactions>. <NADPH> and <ATP> serve different roles even though both connect <light> capture to <carbon fixation>.

= Chloromethane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{Cl}$}
{wiki}

= Methyl chloride
{synonym}

An organochlorine gas with biological as well as possible nonbiological sources. Its specialized biological production is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example.

= Dimethyl sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{CH}_3)_2\mathrm S$}
{wiki}

= DMS
{c}
{synonym}

An organic sulfur gas emitted through biological processing, including marine production associated with sulfur-containing metabolites. It is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example and a possible <atmospheric biosignature gas> whose detectability depends on production and photochemical destruction.

= Ozone
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm O_3$}
{wiki}

A triatomic form of <oxygen>, produced photochemically from <molecular oxygen>. The <ozone layer> absorbs ultraviolet radiation; ozone can trace atmospheric oxygen without being directly emitted by metabolism.

= Nitrous oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

A nitrogen oxide produced substantially by microbial nitrogen cycling on modern <Earth>. Its atmospheric abundance and lifetime make it a candidate <atmospheric biosignature gas>, with context-dependent abiotic alternatives.

= Phosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PH}_3$}
{wiki}

Phosphine is $\mathrm{PH}_3$. Its abundance in giant-planet observable atmospheres can depend on transport from deeper layers faster than local chemical destruction.

= Vanadium(II) oxide
{title2=$\mathrm{VO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Vanadium monoxide
{synonym}

= Titanium monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{TiO}$}
