= Carbon dioxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= CO2
{c}
{synonym}

= Carbon monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Ammonia
{title2=$\mathrm{NH}_3$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methane
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_4$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methanogenesis
{parent=Methane}
{wiki}

Methanogenesis is biological production of <methane> by anaerobic microorganisms. Waterlogged soil and lake sediments can favour it. Warming or thaw changes substrate availability, but production is distinct from atmospheric emission because oxidation and transport intervene.

= Water
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Self-ionization of water
{parent=Water}
{wiki}

Two <water> <molecules> can exchange a <proton>, producing <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <ions>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm{OH}^-$. Even pure <water> therefore supplies <ions>; extreme <dilution> eventually invalidates the <strong acid> assumption that it supplies all the <hydronium>.

= Ice
{parent=Water}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}(s)$}
{wiki}

= Snowflake
{parent=Ice}
{wiki}

A snowflake is an <ice> crystal or an aggregate of <ice> crystals grown in atmospheric conditions. Its morphology depends on vapour supersaturation, transport, <temperature> and crystal anisotropy. Not every snowflake is dendritic.

= Dendritic growth of a snowflake
{parent=Snowflake}

An exposed tip receives vapour more efficiently than a sheltered surface and can amplify its lead, producing <crystal dendrites> and side branches. The planar thermal <solidification> mechanism is an analogy, not a complete vapour-growth model. The <hexagonal ice crystal anisotropy> selects six preferred directions; an isotropic linear instability alone does not select six arms.

= Steady state
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <steady state (chemistry)> has time-independent concentrations because formation and removal rates balance for the species considered. Nonzero reaction fluxes can continue, so a steady state need not be <chemical equilibrium>. A <quasi-steady-state approximation> imposes this balance approximately for a fast intermediate while slower species evolve.

= Physical chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical polarity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

<Chemical polarity> concerns unequal distribution of <electron density> in a <chemical bond> or <molecule>. A <polar molecule> has a permanent <electric dipole moment>; the effects of individual bonds must be combined with their spatial arrangement. In a symmetric linear <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, opposing bond-dipole contributions cancel. In <water>, its <bent molecular geometry> prevents that cancellation, giving a permanent <electric dipole moment>.\a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity][Chemistry 2e, 7.6: geometry and molecular polarity]{ref}

= Polar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

A <polar molecule> has a nonzero permanent <electric dipole moment> because of its internal <electric charge> distribution. It can still have zero total <electric charge>. <Water> is polar; a <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>.

= Nonpolar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

= Nonpolar
{synonym}

A <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>. This distinguishes its charge distribution from the separated partial charges of a polar <molecule>.

= Hydrophilicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophilic
{synonym}

<Hydrophilicity> describes affinity for <water>. Regions with <chemical polarity> or <electric charge> can interact favorably with <water>, including through <hydrogen bonds> where suitable groups are present. <Hydrophilic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the <water> on either side of a <lipid bilayer>. Such affinity does not mean forming <covalent bonds> with every neighboring substance, nor does it require a net negative <electric charge>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/][Alberts et al.: the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

= Amphiphile
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Amphiphilic
{synonym}

= Amphipathic
{synonym}

An <amphiphile> is a <molecule> containing distinct <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions. <Amphiphilic> and <amphipathic> describe this combination. Membrane-forming <phospholipids> are examples; their two regions give different parts of one <molecule> different affinities for <water>. The word describes a structure, not a guarantee of a particular aggregate shape. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303][IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic]{ref}.

= Hydrophobicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophobic
{synonym}

<Hydrophobicity> describes unfavorable mixing with <water>, as illustrated by long <hydrocarbon> regions. The <hydrophobic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the interior of a <lipid bilayer>. This organization is explained by the <hydrophobic effect>, rather than by imagining that chains actively repel <water>. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907][IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction]{ref}.

= Hydrophobic effect
{parent=Hydrophobicity}
{wiki}

The <hydrophobic effect> is the solvent-mediated tendency of <nonpolar> regions to associate in <water>. Exposing a <hydrocarbon> region changes how surrounding <water> <molecules> can arrange and interact; reducing that exposure can lower the total <free energy>. Both <water> and <lipids> enter the accounting. The effect is not an additional <covalent bond> between chains. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907][IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction]{ref}.

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, compare two candidate organizations by the change in <Gibbs free energy>, $\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$, where $H$ is <enthalpy>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. An organization with lower $G$ is favored in this comparison. Reducing exposed <hydrocarbon> area is one contribution; changes in chain packing and the <entropy> of the <lipids> also matter. This explains why <hydrophobicity> alone cannot predict every aggregate shape. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-4-free-energy][Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 16.4: free energy]{ref}.

